{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- selected item list ---' && rg -n -C 8 '\"'^(##?|###?)?'\"\\\\s*(Item\\\\s*)?(15|29|30|33|34)\\\\b|\\\\b(15|29|30|33|34)\\\\b' reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/items_to_address.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- selected feedback ---' && rg -n -C 14 'Item (15|29|30|33|34)|\\\\b(15|29|30|33|34)\\\\b' reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/feedback-the-foreign-policy-impact-of-trade-based-status-ga-2026-09-14.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- local packet ---' && sed -n '1,320p' reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/packets/local_fixes.txt\"","aggregated_output":"--- selected item list ---\n17-| Item | Ponto a trabalhar |\n18-|---:|---|\n19-| 2 | Auditoria cross-country incompletamente reportada: esclarecer a diferença entre os 35 países tratados e os nove casos da tabela de sinais públicos, o destino dos demais países e a definição da categoria `medium`. |\n20-| 4 | Corpus de manchetes da Seção 5 não definido: documentar regra de recuperação, cobertura do arquivo, duplicatas e registros ausentes. |\n21-| 6 | Diagnósticos de interação da Tabela 5 subdefinidos: esclarecer variáveis de exposição, períodos-base, o significado de “× 2008–2009” e os termos de ordem inferior. |\n22-| 7 | Pipeline de classificação da Seção 8.8 incompleto: registrar modelo ou snapshot, configurações, mensagens, lotes, parsing, repetições e normalização dos rótulos. |\n23-| 8 | Validação da classificação não estabelece acurácia na categoria de comércio: esclarecer a amostragem estratificada e avaliar matriz de confusão, precisão, recall e possível variação temporal do erro. |\n24-| 11 | Contrastes temporais da Tabela 4 não têm um estimando comum: examinar diferenças de períodos, horizontes pós-início, contrafactuais e ausência de incerteza para comparar as linhas. |\n25:| 15 | Tabela 23 não expõe os períodos tratados retidos: tornar auditáveis entradas, saídas, episódios curtos excluídos, anos tratados e anos omitidos. |\n26-| 18 | Procedimento de incerteza conflita com os erros-padrão reportados: separar erro-padrão do estimador, rank de placebo por reatribuição e aproximação normal para `p` e intervalos. |\n27-| 20 | Definição dos pesos na exposição do SDiD: esclarecer os pesos positivos para unidades tratadas e períodos pós-tratamento e evitar que a função objetivo pareça eliminar o termo do ATT. |\n28-| 21 | Resultado em nível de voto não definido operacionalmente: especificar a conversão de votos “sim”, “não” e abstenções em distâncias à China e aos Estados Unidos, além do tratamento de ausências e não participação. |\n29-| 22 | Tabela 28 não isola unicamente os efeitos do número de fatores e da fonte do resultado: avaliar a interação entre esses componentes e o problema de escala das medidas de ideal point. |\n30-| 25 | Ausência de efeitos fixos país–domínio: entender melhor se mudanças na disponibilidade de votos e heterogeneidade específica de país e domínio podem afetar a estimativa DDD. **Observação: este item foi selecionado primeiro para entendimento, antes de decidir eventual resposta.** |\n31-| 27 | Figura 8 e texto divergem sobre a estabilidade da China: revisar a caracterização da estabilidade média, da volatilidade anual e da contribuição relativa do Brasil para a distância bilateral. |\n32:| 29 | Figura 2 confunde diferença bruta e ATT do SDiD: distinguir na caption o contraste pós-tratamento ajustado pela diferença prévia da distância vertical bruta entre as séries. |\n33:| 30 | Frase de identificação inverte a hierarquia comercial: corrigir a formulação do estimando para indicar a ascensão da China à primeira posição entre os destinos de exportação do país. |\n34-| 31 | Possível colinearidade das covariáveis com efeitos fixos: verificar o tratamento da distância invariável e do diferencial de poder potencialmente colinear com efeitos de unidade e de tempo. |\n35-| 32 | Caracterização dos pesos temporais do SDiD: revisar a explicação sobre pesos pré-tratamento, aproximação dos resultados pós-tratamento dos controles e informação usada na construção dos pesos. |\n36:| 33 | Inconsistência entre média e mediana: reconciliar a referência a 42% da mediana com o cálculo de 42,2% relativo à média pré-tratamento de `0,647`. |\n37:| 34 | Atribuição incorreta das médias pré-tratamento: corrigir a descrição das médias `0,647` e `0,837` associadas aos resultados BSV e UNGA-DM. |\n38-| 35 | Urdinez et al. (2016) não estuda apenas fluxos comerciais: revisar a afirmação para incorporar investimento direto estrangeiro e empréstimos bancários. |\n39-| 36 | Kastner e Pearson (2021) não estudam apenas comércio: revisar a afirmação para refletir os mecanismos econômicos adicionais discutidos no artigo. |\n40-| 37 | Strüver (2016) é apresentado em direção causal inadequada: conferir a atribuição sobre comércio, interesses econômicos e alinhamento diplomático chinês. |\n41-| 38 | MacDonald e Parent (2021) são associados à definição relacional de status: conferir e corrigir a atribuição, dado o argumento de que status é um atributo, não uma relação. |\n42-\n43-## Fora do escopo desta seleção\n44-\n45-Os comentários detalhados 1, 3, 5, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16, 17, 19, 23, 24, 26 e 28 não foram selecionados nesta etapa.\n--- selected feedback ---\n228-\n229----\n230-\n231-### 11. Table 4 timing contrasts lack a common estimand\n232-\n233-**Status**: [Pending]\n234-\n235-**Quote**:\n236-> Table 4: Brazil SDiD rank-versus-volume timing diagnostics without covariates.\n237-| Timing year | Test role | China rank | Export share (\\%) | Margin (USD bn) | ATT | Inference |\n238-| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n239-| 2003 | Growth/lower-rank promotion | 3 | 6.7 | -12.9 | -0.064 | Point estimate only; no covariates |\n240-| 2004 | China rank-2 threshold | 2 | 7.4 | -14.2 | -0.109 | Point estimate only; no covariates |\n241-| 2005 | Rapid growth without rank 1 | 3 | 7.2 | -16.7 | -0.099 | Point estimate only; no covariates |\n242:| 2009 | Actual rank-1 reversal | 1 | 15.1 | 4.1 | -0.273 | Point estimate only; no covariates |\n243-| 2012 | Later-break falsification | 1 | 18.8 | 14.9 | -0.100 | Point estimate only; no covariates |\n244-\n245-**Feedback**:\n246-The timing rows appear to estimate averages over different calendar periods, post-onset horizons, and fitted SDiD counterfactuals. Because no uncertainty is reported for the differences between rows, their ordering provides suggestive timing evidence but does not establish that the 2009 effect differs statistically from the earlier pseudo-onsets or the later break.\n247-\n248----\n249-\n250-### 12. Timing falsifications do not rule out a gradual confounder\n251-\n252-**Status**: [Pending]\n253-\n254-**Quote**:\n255-> The same timing logic addresses the main Brazilian political confounder. President Lula da Silva took office in 2003 and pursued a more explicit South-South foreign-policy agenda, a shift emphasized in work on Brazilian autonomy, presidential foreign policy, and international engagement (Neto 2011; Mourón and Urdinez 2014; Neto and Malamud 2015; P. Rodrigues, Urdinez, and De Oliveira 2019; Luis L. Schenoni et al. 2022). If that ideological shift were sufficient to explain Brazil-China UNGA convergence, the pseudo-treatment years in 2003 or 2005 should already show comparable movement. They do not.\n256-\n--\n275----\n276-\n277-### 14. Section 6 omits major outcome-measure sensitivity\n278-\n279-**Status**: [Pending]\n280-\n281-**Quote**:\n282-> The preferred cross-country specification is the no-covariate IFE model on the goods-only restricted risk set. Table 7 reports it together with the clean single-entry robustness sample. The estimate is -0.101 ideal-point units (bootstrap SE = 0.039, 95 percent CI [-0.177, - $0.024], \\mathrm{p}=0.010)$. It corresponds to about 14.0 percent of the pre-treatment mean distance to China, or 0.124 standard deviations of the outcome. The estimate is in the theoretically expected negative direction and statistically distinguishable from zero at the 5 percent level. The clean single-entry robustness estimate is -0.122 $(\\mathrm{p}=0.003)$. Appendix Tables 17 and 19 report duration-threshold robustness and sample-coding audits.\n283-\n284-**Feedback**:\n285-The cross-country evidence appears materially outcome-measure-dependent. Appendix Tables 27 and 28 report smaller UNGA-DM estimates with confidence intervals spanning zero, including on identical country-year rows and with two latent factors fixed. Although the predicted negative sign is retained, Section 6's presentation of the beyond-Brazil evidence does not convey this substantial loss of magnitude and precision. Consequently, the Introduction's stability statement is limited to the specified checks and does not establish robustness across UNGA measures.\n286-\n287----\n288-\n289:### 15. Table 23 does not expose retained treatment spells\n290-\n291-**Status**: [Pending]\n292-\n293-**Quote**:\n294-> Table 23 describes the 35 treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set specification. For each country, the table reports the first qualifying China-top goods-export year and the number of treated and untreated country-years retained in the estimation panel.\n295-\n296-Table 23: Treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set cross-country specification.\n297-| Country | ISO3c | Treatment year | Treated years | Pre/other untreated years | First panel year | Last panel year |\n298-| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n299-| Sudan | SDN | 2000 | 6 | 10 | 1990 | 2005 |\n300-| Solomon Islands | SLB | 2003 | 20 | 13 | 1990 | 2022 |\n301-| South Korea | KOR | 2003 | 20 | 12 | 1991 | 2022 |\n302-| Cuba | CUB | 2004 | 18 | 14 | 1990 | 2022 |\n303-| Oman | OMN | 2004 | 19 | 13 | 1990 | 2022 |\n--\n468----\n469-\n470-### 28. Conservative-bias claim in Section 3.3\n471-\n472-**Status**: [Pending]\n473-\n474-**Quote**:\n475-> The AFR item therefore documents broad-metric salience during the same annual treatment window; it does not establish that a strict goodsexport cue preceded the 2009 treatment. This mismatch introduces measurement error into the cross-country treatment indicator. If this error is random and nondifferential with respect to potential voting outcomes and their trends, I would expect attenuation toward zero and would therefore interpret the estimated magnitude as conservative under that assumption.\n476-\n477-**Feedback**:\n478-The claim that metric mismatch would make the estimated magnitude conservative is not generally established for this design. Even random, nondifferential miscoding may shift entry dates, change duration eligibility, or alter the restricted risk set; in a staggered interactive-fixed-effects analysis, those changes can bias the ATT in either direction depending on effect dynamics and treatment timing.\n479-\n480----\n481-\n482:### 29. Figure 2 blurs the raw gap and SDiD ATT\n483-\n484-**Status**: [Pending]\n485-\n486-**Quote**:\n487-> ![](/documents/c44419d6-e784-4221-b6f0-7150c9c2a64a/images/image_002.jpg)\n488-Figure 2: Preferred SDiD fit for Brazil and its synthetic comparison, estimated without covariates. Lower values indicate convergence toward China; the post-treatment gap is the average post-2009 reduced-form estimate. Note: Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China. SE: placebo-based SE with 20,000 replications; p-values reported in the text and table. Window: 1997-2015 annual Brazil series.\n489-\n490-**Feedback**:\n491-Figure 2 plots an unshifted weighted donor path that retains a pre-treatment level gap from Brazil. Consequently, the caption's reference to “the post-treatment gap” is ambiguous: the -0.273 ATT is the level-adjusted post-treatment contrast—equivalently, the post-treatment gap net of the SDiD-weighted pre-treatment gap—not the raw vertical distance between the two series. The figure's arrow appears to depict the adjusted -0.273 contrast correctly, so the issue is limited to the caption's terminology rather than the estimator or visual annotation.\n492-\n493----\n494-\n495:### 30. Identification statement reverses the trade hierarchy\n496-\n497-**Status**: [Pending]\n498-\n499-**Quote**:\n500-> ### 3.2 Identification Strategy\n501-\n502-I want to estimate the causal effect of a country's rise in China's trade hierarchy on its foreign policy. The main direct challenge to causal identification is differentiating it from a continuous foreign-policy approximation as exposure to trade with China increases over\n503-time. Besides that, countries may move closer to China due to changes in domestic political coalitions or foreign-policy doctrine reorientation that coincide in timing with the treatment, because governments already inclined toward China deepen commercial ties before the rank reversal occurs, or due to any other confounding variable.\n504-\n505-**Feedback**:\n506-The opening sentence reverses the treatment hierarchy: the design estimates the effect of China rising to first place among a country's export destinations, not of that country rising within China's trade hierarchy. Although the subsequent definitions and empirical implementation use the intended treatment, this sentence states a different estimand.\n507-\n508----\n509-\n--\n520----\n521-\n522-### 32. Mischaracterization of SDiD time weights\n523-\n524-**Status**: [Pending]\n525-\n526-**Quote**:\n527-> While SDiD assigns larger weights to control units more similar to the treated unit and to time periods more comparable to the pre-treatment period, classic DiD does not use any weights at all.\n528-\n529-**Feedback**:\n530-The description reverses the comparison implemented by SDiD time weights. The optimized weights are placed on pre-treatment periods so that their weighted control-unit outcomes approximate the average post-treatment control outcomes; they are not selected to make periods comparable to the pre-treatment period. Additionally, the text conflates the information used for unit and time weights, incorrectly implying both are constructed solely from pre-treatment information. This is a local methodological misstatement rather than evidence that the estimator was implemented incorrectly.\n531-\n532----\n533-\n534:### 33. Inconsistency on mean vs. median across the manuscript\n535-\n536-**Status**: [Pending]\n537-\n538-**Quote**:\n539-> The paper shows evidence that Brazilian policymakers invoked China's new position to justify greater proximity between the countries, converging voting particularly on human rights issues. Relative to the counterfactual, the estimated reduction in bilateral voting distance is equivalent to 42\\% of the median distance before 2009.\n540-\n541-**Feedback**:\n542-The abstract describes the 42\\% reduction relative to the pre-2009 median, but the body (including the Introduction and Section 4), Table 11, and the appendix calculate 42.2\\% relative to the pre-treatment mean of $0.647$. The stated baseline is therefore inconsistent with the reported calculation.\n543-\n544----\n545-\n546:### 34. Incorrect attribution of pretreatment averages\n547-\n548-**Status**: [Pending]\n549-\n550-**Quote**:\n551-> It is noteworthy that the pretreatment averages are different (0.647 against 0.837) in the original estimation. But the effect measured as a percentage of the pre-treatment average is similar: -42.2 and -39.5 percent.\n552-\n553-**Feedback**:\n554-The phrase “in the original estimation” misattributes the two pretreatment means: Table 24 appears to report 0.647 for the BSV outcome and 0.837 for the UNGA-DM outcome. The comparison and relative-effect calculations are otherwise internally consistent.\n555-\n556----\n--- local packet ---\n# Trechos da referência congelada\n\n19: fontsize: 12pt\n20: abstract: \"\\\\singlespacing  How do countries adjust their foreign policy when a rising power becomes their leading trading partner? We argue that a sufficiently salient and lasting change in trade rankings can shape diplomatic positioning in politically relevant fora, such as the United Nations General Assembly. To study the phenomenon, we focus on Brazil and China's 2009 ranking shift, when China surpassed the United States as Brazil’s largest export destination, using synthetic Difference-in-Differences to estimate the causal effect of the change on Brazil’s voting at the UNGA. The paper shows evidence that Brazilian policymakers invoked China’s new position to justify greater proximity between the countries, converging voting particularly on human rights issues. Relative to the counterfactual, the estimated reduction in bilateral voting distance is equivalent to 42% of the median distance before 2009. To assess whether this pattern extends beyond Brazil, we estimate a latent factor model using data from a panel of countries where China became the leading trading partner between 2000 and 2022. The estimates point in the same direction, although the effects are smaller. These findings suggest that publicly acknowledged changes in economic rank can support diplomatic adjustments in multilateral institutions, with implications for U.S.–China rivalry as their relative commercial positions change.\"\n\n298: ## Identification Strategy\n299: \n300: I want to estimate the causal effect of a country's rise in China's trade hierarchy on its foreign policy. The main direct challenge to causal identification is differentiating it from a continuous foreign-policy approximation as exposure to trade with China increases over time. Besides that, countries may move closer to China due to changes in domestic political coalitions or foreign-policy doctrine reorientation that coincide in timing with the treatment, because governments already inclined toward China deepen commercial ties before the rank reversal occurs, or due to any other confounding variable.\n301: \n\n338: The placebo-based SE is `r sprintf('%1.3f', se_estimate)`, and the conventional two-sided normal-approximation p-value is `r sprintf('%.3f', p_estimate)` with a 95 percent interval [`r sprintf('%.3f', ci_low)`, `r sprintf('%.3f', ci_high)`]. Because the theory predicts convergence, the directionally aligned placebo-in-space rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_one_sided_negative, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_directional)`); the conservative two-sided absolute rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_two_sided_absolute, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_bilateral)`). I report both rather than treating either convention as the sole inferential benchmark. Interpreting these finite-sample ranks as p-values requires placebo assignments to be sufficiently comparable to Brazil; the appendix therefore discloses donor exposure and pre-fit sensitivity. Relative to Brazil's pre-treatment mean distance (`r sprintf('%.3f', mean_br)`), the ATT corresponds to a `r sprintf('%.1f', perc_change)` percent reduction. Appendix Figure \\@ref(fig:appendix-brazil-china-ideal-points) shows that China is comparatively stable, so most of the reduction in absolute distance comes from movement in Brazil's position.\n339: \n340: ```{r plot-sdid, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Preferred SDiD fit for Brazil and its synthetic comparison, estimated without covariates. Lower values indicate convergence toward China; the post-treatment gap is the average post-2009 reduced-form estimate. \", caption_note(unit = \"Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China\", se = \"placebo-based SE with 20,000 replications; p-values reported in the text and table\", window = \"1997-2015 annual Brazil series\")), fig.pos=\"H\", out.width=\"95%\"}\n341: knitr::include_graphics(file.path(\n\n2238: \n2239: The two outcome measures preserve the negative direction of the Brazilian SDiD estimate, but they do not support a blanket claim of robustness across the Brazilian and cross-country designs. Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-sdid-comparison) shows the comparison. Under UNGA-DM, the point estimate is nominally higher than the original in absolute terms (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate)` versus `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate)`), with a lower p-value of `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$p_normal_two_sided)`. The directional rank is the same in both measurements (`r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_one_sided, ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_one_sided)`). The bilateral rank improves from `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_two_sided)` to `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_two_sided)`. It is noteworthy that the pre-treatment averages are different (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$brazil_pre_mean)` against\n2240: `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$brazil_pre_mean)`) in the original estimation. But the effect measured as a percentage of the pre-treatment average is similar: `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)` and `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)` percent. The Brazilian result therefore retains its direction and inferential support under the alternative measure, with a similar relative magnitude. The cross-country comparison below is more sensitive to the outcome source: its IFE estimate is materially smaller and less precise under UNGA-DM. The $2\\times2$ comparison below holds the common window and country-year panel fixed while varying the outcome source and factor count, so the contribution of factor selection can be assessed separately from the outcome-source difference.\n2241: \n2242: ```{r ungadm-sdid-comparison, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
