{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"Os pré-requisitos obrigatórios foram lidos. O item 15 está explicitamente condicionado a uma implementação futura “targets-first”; portanto, nesta rodada vou entregar apenas o esquema revisável e o contrato de validação já especificados, sem fabricar linhas de país-ano. Agora vou confrontar as cinco passagens com o Rmd/PDF congelados e localizar as fontes numéricas existentes para os itens 33–34."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- identity hashes ---' && shasum -a 256 paper_v4.Rmd output/paper_v4.pdf reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.Rmd reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.pdf reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/packets/local_fixes.txt reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/feedback-the-foreign-policy-impact-of-trade-based-status-ga-2026-09-14.md reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/items_to_address.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 29/30 source ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '296,350p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 33/34 source ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '80,140p;590,700p;2225,2255p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- relevant phrases/results files ---' && rg -n -i -C 3 '0\\\\.647|0\\\\.837|42\\\\\\\\?%|42\\\\.2|39\\\\.5|bsv|unga.?dm|pre.?treatment average|pre-treatment mean|Table 24|table 24' paper_v4.Rmd data scripts reports output | sed -n '1,420p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- identity hashes ---' && shasum -a 256 paper_v4.Rmd output/paper_v4.pdf reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.Rmd reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.pdf reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/packets/local_fixes.txt reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/feedback-the-foreign-policy-impact-of-trade-based-status-ga-2026-09-14.md reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/items_to_address.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 29/30 source ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '296,350p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 33/34 source ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '80,140p;590,700p;2225,2255p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- relevant phrases/results files ---' && rg -n -i -C 3 '0\\\\.647|0\\\\.837|42\\\\\\\\?%|42\\\\.2|39\\\\.5|bsv|unga.?dm|pre.?treatment average|pre-treatment mean|Table 24|table 24' paper_v4.Rmd data scripts reports output | sed -n '1,420p'\"","aggregated_output":"paper_v4.Rmd-17-- \\usepackage{float}\npaper_v4.Rmd-18-- \\usepackage{longtable}\npaper_v4.Rmd-19-fontsize: 12pt\npaper_v4.Rmd:20:abstract: \"\\\\singlespacing  How do countries adjust their foreign policy when a rising power becomes their leading trading partner? We argue that a sufficiently salient and lasting change in trade rankings can shape diplomatic positioning in politically relevant fora, such as the United Nations General Assembly. To study the phenomenon, we focus on Brazil and China's 2009 ranking shift, when China surpassed the United States as Brazil’s largest export destination, using synthetic Difference-in-Differences to estimate the causal effect of the change on Brazil’s voting at the UNGA. The paper shows evidence that Brazilian policymakers invoked China’s new position to justify greater proximity between the countries, converging voting particularly on human rights issues. Relative to the counterfactual, the estimated reduction in bilateral voting distance is equivalent to 42% of the median distance before 2009. To assess whether this pattern extends beyond Brazil, we estimate a latent factor model using data from a panel of countries where China became the leading trading partner between 2000 and 2022. The estimates point in the same direction, although the effects are smaller. These findings suggest that publicly acknowledged changes in economic rank can support diplomatic adjustments in multilateral institutions, with implications for U.S.–China rivalry as their relative commercial positions change.\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-21-geometry: margin=2.5cm\npaper_v4.Rmd-22-spacing: double\npaper_v4.Rmd-23-mainfont: Arial\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-146-\npaper_v4.Rmd-147-To estimate the Brazilian foreign-policy alignment pattern, I use synthetic difference-in-differences (SDiD) on annual UNGA ideal-point distance to China, with 2009 marking China's rise to the largest Brazilian export destination. UNGA voting is a standard measure to study alignment with China and other powers, linking votes to bilateral ties, ideology, sanctions, aid, Belt and Road membership, arms transfers, or Chinese economic returns [@potrafke2009; @dreher_jensen2013; @struver2016; @yan_zhou2021; @garcia_herrero_etal2024; @steinert_weyrauch2024; @he_zheng_chen2025; @lektzian_biglaiser2023].\npaper_v4.Rmd-148-\npaper_v4.Rmd:149:In the preferred specification, the estimated reduced-form effect of entry into the new trade-rank condition is a reduction of `r sprintf('%.3f', abs(estimate))` ideal-point units in Brazil's distance to China relative to its synthetic counterfactual, equivalent to `r sprintf('%.1f', perc_change)` percent of its pre-treatment mean, with a one-sided placebo-rank p-value of `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_directional)`.\npaper_v4.Rmd-150-\npaper_v4.Rmd-151-To assess whether a compatible directional pattern appears beyond Brazil and to check the narrower threat posed by explanations tied specifically to Brazil's 2009 calendar year, I extend the analysis to a cross-country panel in which China enters and holds the top goods-export destination position. The cross-country estimates are smaller in magnitude, consistent with a more heterogeneous sample, and point in the same direction as the Brazilian case. The pooled panel estimate is `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_att_intro)` (p-value = `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_intro)`) and is stable across duration thresholds and sample restrictions (p-value between `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_min_intro)` and `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_max_intro)`). The panel evidence identifies a directional pattern associated with entry into the goods-only rank condition; the public-status mechanism is measured directly only in the Brazilian case.\npaper_v4.Rmd-152-\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-335-\npaper_v4.Rmd-336-Figure \\@ref(fig:plot-sdid) presents the preferred SDiD fit, estimated without covariates. The estimated reduced-form effect of entry into the trade-rank condition is negative: the ATT is `r sprintf('%1.3f', estimate)` ideal-point units. Substantively, this is a reduction equivalent to `r sprintf('%.2f', att_sd_br)` standard deviations of Brazil's annual pre-treatment distance to China.\npaper_v4.Rmd-337-\npaper_v4.Rmd:338:The placebo-based SE is `r sprintf('%1.3f', se_estimate)`, and the conventional two-sided normal-approximation p-value is `r sprintf('%.3f', p_estimate)` with a 95 percent interval [`r sprintf('%.3f', ci_low)`, `r sprintf('%.3f', ci_high)`]. Because the theory predicts convergence, the directionally aligned placebo-in-space rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_one_sided_negative, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_directional)`); the conservative two-sided absolute rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_two_sided_absolute, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_bilateral)`). I report both rather than treating either convention as the sole inferential benchmark. Interpreting these finite-sample ranks as p-values requires placebo assignments to be sufficiently comparable to Brazil; the appendix therefore discloses donor exposure and pre-fit sensitivity. Relative to Brazil's pre-treatment mean distance (`r sprintf('%.3f', mean_br)`), the ATT corresponds to a `r sprintf('%.1f', perc_change)` percent reduction. Appendix Figure \\@ref(fig:appendix-brazil-china-ideal-points) shows that China is comparatively stable, so most of the reduction in absolute distance comes from movement in Brazil's position.\npaper_v4.Rmd-339-\npaper_v4.Rmd-340-```{r plot-sdid, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Preferred SDiD fit for Brazil and its synthetic comparison, estimated without covariates. Lower values indicate convergence toward China; the post-treatment gap is the average post-2009 reduced-form estimate. \", caption_note(unit = \"Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China\", se = \"placebo-based SE with 20,000 replications; p-values reported in the text and table\", window = \"1997-2015 annual Brazil series\")), fig.pos=\"H\", out.width=\"95%\"}\npaper_v4.Rmd-341-knitr::include_graphics(file.path(\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-469-  sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_fit_summary$rmspe_pre[1]),\npaper_v4.Rmd-470-  \"Post-treatment / pre-treatment RMSPE\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-471-  sprintf(\"%.2f\", sdid_fit_summary$rmspe_ratio[1]),\npaper_v4.Rmd:472:  \"Brazil's pre-treatment mean distance to China\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-473-  sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_outcome_balance$brazil_pre_mean),\npaper_v4.Rmd-474-  \"Directional rank (convergence toward China)\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-475-  sprintf(\"%d/%d (p = %.3f)\", sdid_all_rank$rank_one_sided_negative,\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-1201-stopifnot(cross_dynamic_n_post == 20L)\npaper_v4.Rmd-1202-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-1203-\npaper_v4.Rmd:1204:The preferred cross-country specification is the no-covariate IFE model on the goods-only restricted risk set. Table \\@ref(tab:cross-country-table) reports it together with the clean single-entry robustness sample. The estimate is `r fmt3(main_cross_s$att)` ideal-point units (bootstrap SE = `r fmt3(main_cross_s$se)`, 95 percent CI [`r fmt3(main_cross_s$ci_lo)`, `r fmt3(main_cross_s$ci_hi)`], p = `r format_p(main_cross_s$p)`). It corresponds to about `r fmt1(main_cross_s$att_rel_pct)` percent of the pre-treatment mean distance to China, or `r fmt3(main_cross_s$att_sd_units)` standard deviations of the outcome. `r sig_sentence(main_cross_s$att, main_cross_s$p)` The clean single-entry robustness estimate is `r fmt3(clean_cross_s$att)` (p = `r format_p(clean_cross_s$p)`). Appendix Tables \\@ref(tab:cross-country-duration-table) and \\@ref(tab:cross-country-audit-table) report duration-threshold robustness and sample-coding audits.\npaper_v4.Rmd-1205-\npaper_v4.Rmd-1206-```{r cross-country-table, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd-1207-cross_tbl <- m2_model_results %>%\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-1299-\npaper_v4.Rmd-1300-# Appendix\npaper_v4.Rmd-1301-\npaper_v4.Rmd:1302:The appendix reports the supporting diagnostics referenced in the main text: outcome and pre-trend series, resolution-level vote diagnostics, SDiD weights and donor-pool checks, the cross-country duration and sample audits, the ChatGPT classification and its validation, and the UNGA-DM measurement robustness.\npaper_v4.Rmd-1303-\npaper_v4.Rmd-1304-## Brazil and China Ideal-Point Series\npaper_v4.Rmd-1305-\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-1517-    `Adj. pre RMSPE` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", rmspe_pre),\npaper_v4.Rmd-1518-    `Adj. post RMSPE` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", rmspe_post),\npaper_v4.Rmd-1519-    `Post/pre adj. RMSPE` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", rmspe_ratio),\npaper_v4.Rmd:1520:    `Pre-treatment mean` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", brazil_pre_treatment_mean),\npaper_v4.Rmd-1521-    `% of pre-mean` = sprintf(\"%.1f\", estimate_as_percent_of_pre_mean)\npaper_v4.Rmd-1522-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-1523-\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2160-           threeparttable = TRUE)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2161-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2162-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2163:## Measurement Robustness: UNGA-DM Ideal Points\npaper_v4.Rmd-2164-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2165:```{r ungadm-inputs, include=FALSE}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2166:ungadm_est_dir <- file.path(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2167:  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"estimation\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2168-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2169:ungadm_post_dir <- file.path(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2170:  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"postreview\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2171-)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2172-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2173:ungadm_sdid_comparison <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2174:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"sdid_comparison_table.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2175-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2176:ungadm_dm_placebo <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2177:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"sdid_dm_placebo_distribution.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2178-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2179:ungadm_rank_criteria <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2180:  file.path(ungadm_post_dir, \"sdid_dm_rank_inference_harmonized.csv\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2181-  show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2182-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2183:ungadm_weights <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2184:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"sdid_unit_weights_bsv_vs_dm.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2185-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2186:ungadm_ife_comparison <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2187:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"ife_comparison_table.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2188-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2189:ungadm_ife_2x2 <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2190:  file.path(ungadm_post_dir, \"ife_2x2_fixed_r.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2191-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2192:ungadm_boot <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2193:  file.path(ungadm_post_dir, \"ife_paired_bootstrap_summary.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2194-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2195:ungadm_unmapped <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2196:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"dm_rows_without_iso3c_mapping.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2197-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2198:ungadm_missing_outcome <- readr::read_csv(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2199:  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"sdid_dm_missing_outcome_rows.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\npaper_v4.Rmd-2200-)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2201-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2202:ungadm_bsv_row <- ungadm_sdid_comparison %>% dplyr::slice(1)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2203:ungadm_dm_row <- ungadm_sdid_comparison %>% dplyr::slice(2)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2204:ungadm_weight_cor <- stats::cor(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2205:  ungadm_weights$unit_weight_bsv, ungadm_weights$unit_weight_dm\npaper_v4.Rmd-2206-)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2207:ungadm_top_dm <- ungadm_weights %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2208-  dplyr::arrange(dplyr::desc(unit_weight_dm)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2209-  dplyr::slice_head(n = 10) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2210-  dplyr::pull(iso3c)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2211:ungadm_top_bsv <- ungadm_weights %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd:2212:  dplyr::arrange(dplyr::desc(unit_weight_bsv)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2213-  dplyr::slice_head(n = 10) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2214-  dplyr::pull(iso3c)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2215:ungadm_top10_overlap <- length(intersect(ungadm_top_dm, ungadm_top_bsv))\npaper_v4.Rmd:2216:ungadm_max_weight_dm <- max(ungadm_weights$unit_weight_dm)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2217:ungadm_max_weight_bsv <- max(ungadm_weights$unit_weight_bsv)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2218:ungadm_boot_selected <- ungadm_boot %>% dplyr::slice(1)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2219:ungadm_boot_common <- ungadm_boot %>% dplyr::slice(2)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2220:ungadm_ife_bsv_common <- ungadm_ife_comparison %>% dplyr::slice(2)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2221:ungadm_ife_dm_common <- ungadm_ife_comparison %>% dplyr::slice(3)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2222:ungadm_ife_bsv_r1 <- ungadm_ife_2x2 %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd:2223:  dplyr::filter(.data$outcome == \"BSV\", .data$r_fixed == 1L)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2224:ungadm_ife_bsv_r2 <- ungadm_ife_2x2 %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd:2225:  dplyr::filter(.data$outcome == \"BSV\", .data$r_fixed == 2L)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2226:ungadm_ife_dm_r1 <- ungadm_ife_2x2 %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd:2227:  dplyr::filter(.data$outcome == \"UNGA-DM\", .data$r_fixed == 1L)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2228:ungadm_ife_dm_r2 <- ungadm_ife_2x2 %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd:2229:  dplyr::filter(.data$outcome == \"UNGA-DM\", .data$r_fixed == 2L)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2230-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2231:ungadm_fmt_ci <- function(lo, hi) sprintf(\"[%.3f, %.3f]\", lo, hi)\npaper_v4.Rmd:2232:ungadm_fmt_rank <- function(rank_value, denom, p_value) {\npaper_v4.Rmd-2233-  sprintf(\"%d/%d (%.3f)\", as.integer(rank_value), as.integer(denom), p_value)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2234-}\npaper_v4.Rmd-2235-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2236-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2237:The main estimations in the paper used the classic ideal-point measurement by BSV [@bailey_etal2017] as the response variable. However, according to @fjelstul_etal2026, the set of resolutions included in the BSV dataset is not complete. They provided a broader dataset, along with re-estimations of ideal points based on the new data. To assess whether the results of the paper change with this new measurement, I reran the main estimations with this new response variable. To make the comparison as close as possible, I held the treatment definition, donor and risk-set rules, covariate structure, and inference conventions fixed. The UNGA-DM series has a shorter time window, and the SDiD unit and time weights and the IFE factor count are re-estimated by their algorithms in response to the outcome series. These induced adjustments mean that the comparison evaluates the robustness of the analysis package to the alternative outcome; it is not a pure measurement-only perturbation of an otherwise identical fitted counterfactual. It is also important to note that `r nrow(ungadm_unmapped)` country-session rows had no ISO3c match and `r nrow(ungadm_missing_outcome)` SDiD rows had no outcome in the alternative measurement.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2238-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2239:The two outcome measures preserve the negative direction of the Brazilian SDiD estimate, but they do not support a blanket claim of robustness across the Brazilian and cross-country designs. Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-sdid-comparison) shows the comparison. Under UNGA-DM, the point estimate is nominally higher than the original in absolute terms (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate)` versus `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate)`), with a lower p-value of `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$p_normal_two_sided)`. The directional rank is the same in both measurements (`r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_one_sided, ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_one_sided)`). The bilateral rank improves from `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_two_sided)` to `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_two_sided)`. It is noteworthy that the pre-treatment averages are different (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$brazil_pre_mean)` against\npaper_v4.Rmd:2240:`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$brazil_pre_mean)`) in the original estimation. But the effect measured as a percentage of the pre-treatment average is similar: `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)` and `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)` percent. The Brazilian result therefore retains its direction and inferential support under the alternative measure, with a similar relative magnitude. The cross-country comparison below is more sensitive to the outcome source: its IFE estimate is materially smaller and less precise under UNGA-DM. The $2\\times2$ comparison below holds the common window and country-year panel fixed while varying the outcome source and factor count, so the contribution of factor selection can be assessed separately from the outcome-source difference.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2241-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2242:```{r ungadm-sdid-comparison, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2243:ungadm_comparison_tbl <- tibble::tibble(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2244-  measure = c(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2245-    \"ATT\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2246-    \"Placebo standard error\",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2250-    \"Two-sided absolute placebo rank (p)\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2251-    \"Intercept-adjusted pre-treatment RMSPE\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2252-    \"Brazil pre-treatment outcome mean\",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2253:    \"ATT as percent of pre-treatment mean\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2254-  ),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2255:  bsv = c(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2256:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2257:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$se_placebo),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2258:    ungadm_fmt_ci(ungadm_bsv_row$ci_95_low, ungadm_bsv_row$ci_95_high),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2259:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$p_normal_two_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2260:    ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_one_sided,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2261:                    ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2262:                    ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_one_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2263:    ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_two_sided,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2264:                    ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2265:                    ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_two_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2266:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$rmspe_pre),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2267:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_bsv_row$brazil_pre_mean),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2268:    sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_bsv_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2269-  ),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2270-  dm = c(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2271:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2272:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$se_placebo),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2273:    ungadm_fmt_ci(ungadm_dm_row$ci_95_low, ungadm_dm_row$ci_95_high),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2274:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$p_normal_two_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2275:    ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_one_sided,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2276:                    ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2277:                    ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_one_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2278:    ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_two_sided,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2279:                    ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2280:                    ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_two_sided),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2281:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$rmspe_pre),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2282:    sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$brazil_pre_mean),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2283:    sprintf(\"%.1f\", ungadm_dm_row$estimate_pct_pre_mean)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2284-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2285-)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2286-\npaper_v4.Rmd-2287-knitr::kable(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2288:  ungadm_comparison_tbl,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2289-  format = \"latex\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2290-  booktabs = TRUE,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2291-  caption = \"Brazil SDiD under two sources of UNGA ideal points. Both columns use the same panel, the same donor pool, and the same placebo standard error settings; the outcome series is the only input that differs, and the synthetic-control weights are re-estimated from it.\",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2298-  kableExtra::footnote(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2299-    general = paste0(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2300-      \"Estimates are the post-2009 gap in absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China, 1997-2015 annual panel, no covariates. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2301:      \"Placebo SEs use \", format(ungadm_bsv_row$se_replications, big.mark = \",\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2302:      \" replications and seed \", ungadm_bsv_row$se_seed, \" in both columns. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2303-      \"Placebo-in-space ranks involve no random draws and are exactly reproducible.\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2304-    ),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2305-    general_title = \"Note: \",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2308-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2309-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2310-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2311:Figure \\@ref(fig:ungadm-placebo-distribution) reports the placebo-in-space distribution for the UNGA-DM SDiD fit. It is close to the distribution obtained under the original outcome. The directional placebo rank is unchanged under the alternative measure (`r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_one_sided, ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_one_sided)` in both columns), while the bilateral absolute rank improves from `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_bsv_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_bsv_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_bsv_row$p_rank_two_sided)` to `r ungadm_fmt_rank(ungadm_dm_row$rank_two_sided, ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_two_sided)`. Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-sdid-comparison) reports this cross-measure comparison, and Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-rank-criteria) reports the donor-exclusion checks under UNGA-DM.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2312-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2313:```{r ungadm-placebo-distribution, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Placebo-in-space distribution under the UNGA-DM outcome, with Brazil marked. Brazil's estimate is the \", scales::ordinal(ungadm_dm_row$rank_one_sided), \" most negative of \", ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, \" assignments (directional p = \", sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_one_sided), \"); the two-sided absolute rank is \", ungadm_dm_row$rank_two_sided, \"/\", ungadm_dm_row$rank_denominator, \" (p = \", sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_dm_row$p_rank_two_sided), \"). Negative values indicate convergence toward China. \", caption_note(unit = \"Estimated post-2009 gap in absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China, UNGA-DM measure\", window = \"1997-2015 annual SDiD panel\")), fig.pos=\"H\", fig.width=7, fig.asp=0.5}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2314:ungadm_placebo_valid <- ungadm_dm_placebo %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2315-  dplyr::filter(status == \"estimated\", !is.na(estimate)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2316-  dplyr::mutate(is_brazil = iso3c == \"BRA\")\npaper_v4.Rmd:2317:ungadm_brazil_estimate <- ungadm_placebo_valid$estimate[ungadm_placebo_valid$is_brazil]\npaper_v4.Rmd-2318-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2319:ggplot2::ggplot(ungadm_placebo_valid, ggplot2::aes(x = estimate, fill = is_brazil)) +\npaper_v4.Rmd-2320-  ggplot2::geom_histogram(bins = 30, colour = \"white\", linewidth = 0.2) +\npaper_v4.Rmd:2321:  ggplot2::geom_vline(xintercept = ungadm_brazil_estimate, linetype = \"dashed\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2322-                      colour = \"#b2182b\", linewidth = 0.6) +\npaper_v4.Rmd-2323-  ggplot2::annotate(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2324:    \"text\", x = ungadm_brazil_estimate, y = Inf,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2325:    label = paste0(\" Brazil: \", sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_brazil_estimate)),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2326-    hjust = 0, vjust = 1.6, size = 3.2, colour = \"#b2182b\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2327-  ) +\npaper_v4.Rmd-2328-  ggplot2::scale_fill_manual(values = c(`TRUE` = \"#b2182b\", `FALSE` = \"grey70\"),\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2333-  ggplot2::theme(panel.grid.minor = ggplot2::element_blank())\npaper_v4.Rmd-2334-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2335-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2336:```{r ungadm-rank-criteria, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2337:ungadm_rank_tbl <- ungadm_rank_criteria %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2338-  dplyr::transmute(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2339-    comparison = comparison_set,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2340-    excluded = dplyr::if_else(is.na(excluded_units) | excluded_units == \"\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2341-                              \"--\", gsub(\";\", \", \", excluded_units)),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2342:    directional = ungadm_fmt_rank(rank_one_sided_negative, denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2343-                                  p_rank_one_sided_negative),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2344:    two_sided = ungadm_fmt_rank(rank_two_sided_absolute, denominator,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2345-                                p_rank_two_sided_absolute)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2346-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2347-\npaper_v4.Rmd-2348-knitr::kable(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2349:  ungadm_rank_tbl,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2350-  format = \"latex\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2351-  booktabs = TRUE,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2352:  caption = \"Placebo rank inference under the UNGA-DM outcome, by donor-exclusion criterion.\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2353-  col.names = c(\"Comparison set\", \"Excluded donors\", \"Directional rank (p)\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2354-                \"Two-sided rank (p)\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2355-  align = \"llcc\"\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2369-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2370-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2371-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2372:Donor weights are estimated separately for each fit, so they are not identical across outcomes. Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-donor-weights) reports how much they change. Weight is spread among donors in a similar way in both fits: `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_max_weight_dm)` under UNGA-DM and `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_max_weight_bsv)` under BSV. The correlation of the donor pool weights is moderate (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_weight_cor)`), but among the top ten donors, `r ungadm_top10_overlap` are the same. The donor mix therefore shifts moderately across outcomes. For Brazil, the negative estimate and similar relative magnitude are preserved, but the two fits do not use identical counterfactual weights; this comparison does not establish stability of the cross-country result.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2373-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2374:```{r ungadm-donor-weights, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2375:ungadm_weights_tbl <- ungadm_weights %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2376-  dplyr::arrange(dplyr::desc(unit_weight_dm)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2377-  dplyr::slice_head(n = 10) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2378-  dplyr::transmute(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2379-    iso3c,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2380-    dm = sprintf(\"%.3f\", unit_weight_dm),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2381:    bsv = sprintf(\"%.3f\", unit_weight_bsv)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2382-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2383-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2384:ungadm_top10_dm <- ungadm_weights %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2385-  dplyr::arrange(dplyr::desc(unit_weight_dm)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2386-  dplyr::slice_head(n = 10) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2387-  dplyr::summarise(total = sum(unit_weight_dm)) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2388-  dplyr::pull(total)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2389-\npaper_v4.Rmd-2390-knitr::kable(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2391:  ungadm_weights_tbl,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2392-  format = \"latex\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2393-  booktabs = TRUE,\npaper_v4.Rmd:2394:  caption = \"Ten largest synthetic-control donor weights under the UNGA-DM fit, with the weight the same donor receives under the main outcome.\",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2395:  col.names = c(\"Donor\", \"UNGA-DM weight\", \"BSV weight\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2396-  align = \"lcc\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2397-) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2398-  kableExtra::kable_styling(\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2401-  kableExtra::footnote(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2402-    general = paste0(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2403-      \"Weights are normalized over the full donor pool of \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2404:      nrow(ungadm_weights), \" units rather than over the displayed donors. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2405:      \"The ten donors shown carry \", sprintf(\"%.1f\", 100 * ungadm_top10_dm),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2406:      \" percent of total weight under the UNGA-DM fit. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2407-      \"Across all donors, the correlation between the two weight vectors is \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2408:      sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_weight_cor), \", and \", ungadm_top10_overlap,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2409-      \" of the ten largest donors are shared by the two fits.\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2410-    ),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2411-    general_title = \"Note: \",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2414-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2415-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2416-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2417:Regarding the panel regression, Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-ife-window) shows the IFE estimation in the same time window for both outcomes, and the original estimation with the larger time window. The estimate retains the negative sign but is smaller and less precise under UNGA-DM:\npaper_v4.Rmd:2418:`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_dm_common$att)` with p =\npaper_v4.Rmd:2419:`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_dm_common$p)`. However, the models are not the same, since the number of latent factors chosen by cross-validation is different: `r ungadm_ife_bsv_common$r_cv` under BSV and `r ungadm_ife_dm_common$r_cv` under UNGA-DM. The $2\\times2$ comparison below holds the common window and country-year panel fixed while crossing the outcome source with one versus two latent factors, allowing the factor-count component to be assessed separately.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2420-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2421:```{r ungadm-ife-window, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2422:ungadm_ife_tbl <- ungadm_ife_comparison %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2423-  dplyr::transmute(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2424-    specification = variant,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2425-    att = sprintf(\"%.3f\", att),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2426-    se = sprintf(\"%.3f\", se),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2427:    ci = ungadm_fmt_ci(ci_lo, ci_hi),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2428-    p = sprintf(\"%.3f\", p),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2429-    r_cv = as.integer(r_cv),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2430-    n_obs = format(n_obs, big.mark = \",\")\npaper_v4.Rmd-2431-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2432-\npaper_v4.Rmd-2433-knitr::kable(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2434:  ungadm_ife_tbl,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2435-  format = \"latex\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2436-  booktabs = TRUE,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2437-  caption = \"Cross-country interactive fixed-effects panel under the two outcome sources, in a common window with identical rows.\",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2446-  kableExtra::footnote(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2447-    general = paste0(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2448-      \"All rows use the goods-only status-current restricted risk set with \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2449:      ungadm_ife_bsv_common$n_treated, \" treated and \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2450:      ungadm_ife_bsv_common$n_control, \" control countries. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2451-      \"The common-window rows keep exactly the same country-years in both outcome sources. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2452:      \"Confidence intervals come from \", format(ungadm_ife_bsv_common$nboots, big.mark = \",\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2453-      \" bootstrap replications.\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2454-    ),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2455-    general_title = \"Note: \",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2458-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2459-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2460-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2461:Table \\@ref(tab:ungadm-ife-2x2) holds the common window and country-year panel fixed and crosses the two outcome sources with one and two latent factors. This $2\\times2$ design isolates the factor-count component of the comparison. Holding the outcome fixed, moving from one to two factors makes the ATT more negative (larger in absolute value): from `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_bsv_r1$att)` to `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_bsv_r2$att)` under BSV, and from `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_dm_r1$att)` to `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_dm_r2$att)` under UNGA-DM. Cross-validation selects `r ungadm_ife_bsv_common$r_cv` factors under BSV and `r ungadm_ife_dm_common$r_cv` under UNGA-DM. The one-factor UNGA-DM fit selected by cross-validation has the same negative sign and substantially overlapping uncertainty with the fixed-two-factor fit, although its point estimate is less negative; factor selection therefore changes magnitude without reversing the qualitative pattern. Holding the factor count at two still leaves a smaller UNGA-DM estimate (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_dm_r2$att)`) than the BSV estimate (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_ife_bsv_r2$att)`), so the outcome source remains relevant after the factor-count component is isolated.\npaper_v4.Rmd-2462-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2463:```{r ungadm-ife-2x2, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\npaper_v4.Rmd:2464:ungadm_2x2_tbl <- ungadm_ife_2x2 %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2465-  dplyr::arrange(r_fixed, outcome) %>%\npaper_v4.Rmd-2466-  dplyr::transmute(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2467-    outcome,\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2469-    cv = dplyr::if_else(cv_selected, \"Yes\", \"No\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2470-    att = sprintf(\"%.3f\", att),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2471-    se = sprintf(\"%.3f\", se),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2472:    ci = ungadm_fmt_ci(ci_lo, ci_hi),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2473-    p = sprintf(\"%.3f\", p)\npaper_v4.Rmd-2474-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2475-\npaper_v4.Rmd-2476-knitr::kable(\npaper_v4.Rmd:2477:  ungadm_2x2_tbl,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2478-  format = \"latex\",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2479-  booktabs = TRUE,\npaper_v4.Rmd-2480-  caption = \"Outcome source by number of latent factors, holding the panel fixed.\",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2489-    general = paste0(\npaper_v4.Rmd-2490-      \"Same common window and identical rows as the previous table. \",\npaper_v4.Rmd-2491-      \"Paired bootstrap on the difference between the two ATTs, \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2492:      format(ungadm_boot_selected$n_valid, big.mark = \",\"),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2493-      \" replications and no failures: \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2494:      sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_boot_selected$observed_diff),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2495:      \" (percentile p = \", sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_boot_selected$p_two_sided_percentile),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2496-      \") under procedure-selected factor counts, and \",\npaper_v4.Rmd:2497:      sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_boot_common$observed_diff),\npaper_v4.Rmd:2498:      \" (percentile p = \", sprintf(\"%.3f\", ungadm_boot_common$p_two_sided_percentile),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2499-      \") with common factors.\"\npaper_v4.Rmd-2500-    ),\npaper_v4.Rmd-2501-    general_title = \"Note: \",\n--\npaper_v4.Rmd-2504-  )\npaper_v4.Rmd-2505-```\npaper_v4.Rmd-2506-\npaper_v4.Rmd:2507:To sum up, the Brazilian SDiD result retains its negative direction and inferential support across the two outcome measurements, with similar relative magnitude but a different absolute scale. The $2\\times2$ IFE comparison shows that two factors produce a more negative ATT under either outcome, while the one-factor choice selected by cross-validation for UNGA-DM remains qualitatively close to the two-factor fit, with the same sign and overlapping uncertainty. Even with two factors held fixed, the UNGA-DM estimate remains smaller than the BSV estimate, so the outcome metric contributes beyond factor selection. The paired test does not detect a statistically significant difference between the ATTs, but this non-rejection does not establish equivalence and may reflect uncertainty.\n--\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh-35-[ -f \"$STATE\" ] || printf 'batch\\tstatus\\tfinished_at\\tduration\\n' > \"$STATE\"\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh-36-\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh-37-# Ordered batch list, with rough wall-clock estimates for planning a sitting.\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh:38:BATCHES=(prep data se_1 se_2 se_3 core diagnostics table5 ungadm ungadm_post render extended legacy)\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh-39-declare -a ESTIMATES=(\nscripts/run_rebuild_batch.sh-40-  \"~10 m