{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- all mean/median/42 references in active Rmd only ---' && rg -n -i -C 2 'median|mean distance|pre-treatment mean|42\\\\\\\\?%|42\\\\.2|39\\\\.5|0\\\\.647|0\\\\.837' paper_v4.Rmd && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 15 current source lines ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '2100,2170p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- Table 23 extracted source in current Rmd ---' && rg -n -C 18 'cross-country-treated|Treated countries|treated-country|Table 23|tab:.*treated|Treatment year|First panel year' paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '1,260p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- table 23 in frozen PDF text ---' && rg -n -C 12 'Treated countries in the main|First panel year|Sudan|Solomon Islands|Treatment year' reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.txt | tail -120\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '--- all mean/median/42 references in active Rmd only ---' && rg -n -i -C 2 'median|mean distance|pre-treatment mean|42\\\\\\\\?%|42\\\\.2|39\\\\.5|0\\\\.647|0\\\\.837' paper_v4.Rmd && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- item 15 current source lines ---' && nl -ba paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '2100,2170p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- Table 23 extracted source in current Rmd ---' && rg -n -C 18 'cross-country-treated|Treated countries|treated-country|Table 23|tab:.*treated|Treatment year|First panel year' paper_v4.Rmd | sed -n '1,260p' && printf '%s\\\\n' '--- table 23 in frozen PDF text ---' && rg -n -C 12 'Treated countries in the main|First panel year|Sudan|Solomon Islands|Treatment year' reports/refine_ink_review_paper_v4_2026-09-14/execution/baseline/paper_v4.txt | tail -120\"","aggregated_output":"--- all mean/median/42 references in active Rmd only ---\n18-- \\usepackage{longtable}\n19-fontsize: 12pt\n20:abstract: \"\\\\singlespacing  How do countries adjust their foreign policy when a rising power becomes their leading trading partner? We argue that a sufficiently salient and lasting change in trade rankings can shape diplomatic positioning in politically relevant fora, such as the United Nations General Assembly. To study the phenomenon, we focus on Brazil and China's 2009 ranking shift, when China surpassed the United States as Brazil’s largest export destination, using synthetic Difference-in-Differences to estimate the causal effect of the change on Brazil’s voting at the UNGA. The paper shows evidence that Brazilian policymakers invoked China’s new position to justify greater proximity between the countries, converging voting particularly on human rights issues. Relative to the counterfactual, the estimated reduction in bilateral voting distance is equivalent to 42% of the median distance before 2009. To assess whether this pattern extends beyond Brazil, we estimate a latent factor model using data from a panel of countries where China became the leading trading partner between 2000 and 2022. The estimates point in the same direction, although the effects are smaller. These findings suggest that publicly acknowledged changes in economic rank can support diplomatic adjustments in multilateral institutions, with implications for U.S.–China rivalry as their relative commercial positions change.\"\n21-geometry: margin=2.5cm\n22-spacing: double\n--\n147-To estimate the Brazilian foreign-policy alignment pattern, I use synthetic difference-in-differences (SDiD) on annual UNGA ideal-point distance to China, with 2009 marking China's rise to the largest Brazilian export destination. UNGA voting is a standard measure to study alignment with China and other powers, linking votes to bilateral ties, ideology, sanctions, aid, Belt and Road membership, arms transfers, or Chinese economic returns [@potrafke2009; @dreher_jensen2013; @struver2016; @yan_zhou2021; @garcia_herrero_etal2024; @steinert_weyrauch2024; @he_zheng_chen2025; @lektzian_biglaiser2023].\n148-\n149:In the preferred specification, the estimated reduced-form effect of entry into the new trade-rank condition is a reduction of `r sprintf('%.3f', abs(estimate))` ideal-point units in Brazil's distance to China relative to its synthetic counterfactual, equivalent to `r sprintf('%.1f', perc_change)` percent of its pre-treatment mean, with a one-sided placebo-rank p-value of `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_directional)`.\n150-\n151-To assess whether a compatible directional pattern appears beyond Brazil and to check the narrower threat posed by explanations tied specifically to Brazil's 2009 calendar year, I extend the analysis to a cross-country panel in which China enters and holds the top goods-export destination position. The cross-country estimates are smaller in magnitude, consistent with a more heterogeneous sample, and point in the same direction as the Brazilian case. The pooled panel estimate is `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_att_intro)` (p-value = `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_intro)`) and is stable across duration thresholds and sample restrictions (p-value between `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_min_intro)` and `r sprintf('%.3f', panel_p_max_intro)`). The panel evidence identifies a directional pattern associated with entry into the goods-only rank condition; the public-status mechanism is measured directly only in the Brazilian case.\n--\n336-Figure \\@ref(fig:plot-sdid) presents the preferred SDiD fit, estimated without covariates. The estimated reduced-form effect of entry into the trade-rank condition is negative: the ATT is `r sprintf('%1.3f', estimate)` ideal-point units. Substantively, this is a reduction equivalent to `r sprintf('%.2f', att_sd_br)` standard deviations of Brazil's annual pre-treatment distance to China.\n337-\n338:The placebo-based SE is `r sprintf('%1.3f', se_estimate)`, and the conventional two-sided normal-approximation p-value is `r sprintf('%.3f', p_estimate)` with a 95 percent interval [`r sprintf('%.3f', ci_low)`, `r sprintf('%.3f', ci_high)`]. Because the theory predicts convergence, the directionally aligned placebo-in-space rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_one_sided_negative, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_directional)`); the conservative two-sided absolute rank is `r sprintf('%d/%d', sdid_main_summary$rank_two_sided_absolute, sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)` (p = `r sprintf('%.3f', p_rank_bilateral)`). I report both rather than treating either convention as the sole inferential benchmark. Interpreting these finite-sample ranks as p-values requires placebo assignments to be sufficiently comparable to Brazil; the appendix therefore discloses donor exposure and pre-fit sensitivity. Relative to Brazil's pre-treatment mean distance (`r sprintf('%.3f', mean_br)`), the ATT corresponds to a `r sprintf('%.1f', perc_change)` percent reduction. Appendix Figure \\@ref(fig:appendix-brazil-china-ideal-points) shows that China is comparatively stable, so most of the reduction in absolute distance comes from movement in Brazil's position.\n339-\n340-```{r plot-sdid, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Preferred SDiD fit for Brazil and its synthetic comparison, estimated without covariates. Lower values indicate convergence toward China; the post-treatment gap is the average post-2009 reduced-form estimate. \", caption_note(unit = \"Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China\", se = \"placebo-based SE with 20,000 replications; p-values reported in the text and table\", window = \"1997-2015 annual Brazil series\")), fig.pos=\"H\", out.width=\"95%\"}\n--\n470-  \"Post-treatment / pre-treatment RMSPE\",\n471-  sprintf(\"%.2f\", sdid_fit_summary$rmspe_ratio[1]),\n472:  \"Brazil's pre-treatment mean distance to China\",\n473-  sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_outcome_balance$brazil_pre_mean),\n474-  \"Directional rank (convergence toward China)\",\n--\n1202-```\n1203-\n1204:The preferred cross-country specification is the no-covariate IFE model on the goods-only restricted risk set. Table \\@ref(tab:cross-country-table) reports it together with the clean single-entry robustness sample. The estimate is `r fmt3(main_cross_s$att)` ideal-point units (bootstrap SE = `r fmt3(main_cross_s$se)`, 95 percent CI [`r fmt3(main_cross_s$ci_lo)`, `r fmt3(main_cross_s$ci_hi)`], p = `r format_p(main_cross_s$p)`). It corresponds to about `r fmt1(main_cross_s$att_rel_pct)` percent of the pre-treatment mean distance to China, or `r fmt3(main_cross_s$att_sd_units)` standard deviations of the outcome. `r sig_sentence(main_cross_s$att, main_cross_s$p)` The clean single-entry robustness estimate is `r fmt3(clean_cross_s$att)` (p = `r format_p(clean_cross_s$p)`). Appendix Tables \\@ref(tab:cross-country-duration-table) and \\@ref(tab:cross-country-audit-table) report duration-threshold robustness and sample-coding audits.\n1205-\n1206-```{r cross-country-table, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\n--\n1518-    `Adj. post RMSPE` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", rmspe_post),\n1519-    `Post/pre adj. RMSPE` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", rmspe_ratio),\n1520:    `Pre-treatment mean` = sprintf(\"%.3f\", brazil_pre_treatment_mean),\n1521-    `% of pre-mean` = sprintf(\"%.1f\", estimate_as_percent_of_pre_mean)\n1522-  )\n--\n2251-    \"Intercept-adjusted pre-treatment RMSPE\",\n2252-    \"Brazil pre-treatment outcome mean\",\n2253:    \"ATT as percent of pre-treatment mean\"\n2254-  ),\n2255-  bsv = c(\n--- item 15 current source lines ---\n  2100\t  kableExtra::kable_styling(\n  2101\t    latex_options = c(\"HOLD_position\", \"scale_down\"),\n  2102\t    font_size = 9\n  2103\t  ) %>%\n  2104\t  kableExtra::footnote(\n  2105\t    general = sub(\n  2106\t      \"^Note: \",\n  2107\t      \"\",\n  2108\t      caption_note(\n  2109\t        unit = \"headline-level counts and accuracy (percent) by category\",\n  2110\t        window = \"stratified validation sample from the 2000-2014 headline corpus\"\n  2111\t      )\n  2112\t    ),\n  2113\t    general_title = \"Note: \",\n  2114\t    escape = FALSE,\n  2115\t    threeparttable = TRUE\n  2116\t  )\n  2117\t```\n  2118\t\n  2119\tThe overall accuracy is `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", overall_acc)` percent. The lowest agreement is in the \"`r lowest_category`\" category (`r sprintf(\"%.1f\", lowest_acc)` percent), where several headlines were manually reclassified as \"diplomacy.\" The category most central to the mechanism, \"china-brazil trade,\" achieved `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", trade_acc)` percent agreement. Related bilateral categories had lower but still usable agreement: `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", relations_acc)` percent for \"china-brazil relations\" and `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", diplomacy_acc)` percent for \"diplomacy.\"\n  2120\t\n  2121\t## Cross-Country: Goods-Only Treated Countries\n  2122\t\n  2123\tTable \\@ref(tab:table-treated-appendix) describes the `r n_treated_abs` treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set specification. For each country, the table reports the first qualifying China-top goods-export year and the number of treated and untreated country-years retained in the estimation panel.\n  2124\t\n  2125\t```{r table-treated-appendix, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE}\n  2126\tlibrary(kableExtra)\n  2127\t\n  2128\ttreated_tbl <- m2_unit_summary %>%\n  2129\t  dplyr::filter(\n  2130\t    min_duration_years == 5L,\n  2131\t    sample == \"risk_set_restricted\",\n  2132\t    ever_treated\n  2133\t  ) %>%\n  2134\t  dplyr::arrange(first_treat, country_name) %>%\n  2135\t  dplyr::transmute(\n  2136\t    country = country_name,\n  2137\t    iso3c,\n  2138\t    first_treat_year = as.integer(first_treat),\n  2139\t    treated_years,\n  2140\t    untreated_years,\n  2141\t    first_year_in_panel,\n  2142\t    last_year_in_panel\n  2143\t  )\n  2144\t\n  2145\tkable(treated_tbl,\n  2146\t      format = \"latex\",\n  2147\t      booktabs = TRUE,\n  2148\t      digits = 3,\n  2149\t      caption = \"Treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set cross-country specification.\",\n  2150\t      col.names = c(\"Country\", \"ISO3c\", \"Treatment year\", \"Treated years\",\n  2151\t                     \"Pre/other untreated years\", \"First panel year\", \"Last panel year\"),\n  2152\t      align = \"llccccc\") %>%\n  2153\t  kable_styling(latex_options = c(\"hold_position\", \"scale_down\"),\n  2154\t                font_size = 9) %>%\n  2155\t  footnote(general = paste0(\"Ranks are computed from goods exports only. \",\n  2156\t                            \"The restricted risk set keeps clean pre-entry non-China-top years and qualifying treated years for treated countries. \",\n  2157\t                            \"Treatment year is the first retained year of a qualifying China-top goods-export period.\"),\n  2158\t           general_title = \"Note: \",\n  2159\t           escape = FALSE,\n  2160\t           threeparttable = TRUE)\n  2161\t```\n  2162\t\n  2163\t## Measurement Robustness: UNGA-DM Ideal Points\n  2164\t\n  2165\t```{r ungadm-inputs, include=FALSE}\n  2166\tungadm_est_dir <- file.path(\n  2167\t  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"estimation\"\n  2168\t)\n  2169\tungadm_post_dir <- file.path(\n  2170\t  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"postreview\"\n--- Table 23 extracted source in current Rmd ---\n296-```\n297-\n298-## Identification Strategy\n299-\n300-I want to estimate the causal effect of a country's rise in China's trade hierarchy on its foreign policy. The main direct challenge to causal identification is differentiating it from a continuous foreign-policy approximation as exposure to trade with China increases over time. Besides that, countries may move closer to China due to changes in domestic political coalitions or foreign-policy doctrine reorientation that coincide in timing with the treatment, because governments already inclined toward China deepen commercial ties before the rank reversal occurs, or due to any other confounding variable.\n301-\n302-To address these issues, I use Synthetic Difference-in-Differences (SDiD). Like the Synthetic Control Method (SCM), it constructs a weighted counterfactual from a donor pool to estimate the causal effect, using weights to approximate the counterfactual in ways that DiD cannot. Unlike the SCM and similar to the DiD, it estimates unit fixed effects, but with different weights. This combines both methods into a single one. As a result, violations of (unweighted average) parallel trends do not mean violations of weighted parallel trends. And, unique to the method, it weights the time periods, choosing the ones most comparable pre- and post-treatment. In other words, the weights pool observations to reduce bias in a latent factor model, even when global parallel trends are not valid. @arkhangelsky_etal2021 show SDiD is consistent under this latent factor model when weights converge adequately.\n303-\n304-The SDiD design estimates the reduced-form effect of entry into this trade-rank condition on UNGA distance to China.\n305-\n306-The preferred specification uses no covariates because time-varying post-2009 values may induce post-treatment bias. A model specification with time-varying variables uses trade, power, ideology, macroeconomic stress, and institutional variables to control for potential confounding. The similarity of the estimates indicates low sensitivity to post-treatment adjustment, suggesting the results are not driven by the covariates. Commodity composition and price interactions are also treated as robustness or mechanism diagnostics, especially because the 2008--2009 interaction includes the first treated year.\n307-\n308-To compute the uncertainty of estimates, I use a one-sided placebo-in-space rank of the Brazil estimate within the distribution of placebo estimates obtained by reassigning treatment to each unit from the donor pool. For robustness, I also report the standard error calculated from the normal approximation native to the SDiD estimator. I use a one-sided hypothesis test, since the theory is directional (a treated unit should move its ideal point toward China), and report the two-sided test as a conservative sensitivity assessment.\n309-\n310-Two features of rank-based inference with a single treated unit are noteworthy. First, with only `r sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator` countries, the most extreme possible result would be Brazil having the largest effect, yielding a p-value floor of approximately `r sprintf('%.3f', 1 / sdid_main_summary$rank_denominator)`. Second, the two-sided absolute rank counts large placebo effects of either sign against Brazil, including positive placebo effects that do not represent the error relevant to a directional hypothesis. I apply the same convention throughout the paper to every placebo-based diagnostic.\n311-\n312-## Cross-Country Regression\n313-\n314:Notwithstanding my best effort to address potential rival explanations for the observed reduced-form causal effect of the change in 2009, there is only so much one can do with a single treated case. Thus, I also consider a broader panel of countries in which China became the number-one export destination, estimated with a latent interactive fixed-effects regression [@liu2024practical]. Interactive fixed effects allow countries to load differently on common latent shocks. Treated countries enter China-top status in different years, so a Brazil-specific 2009 shock cannot mechanically generate the pooled result.\n315-\n316-This approach, however, faces some challenges that must be addressed or at least recognized. The conceptual treatment, as mentioned, is status, not the rank. However, for each country what matters is not necessarily being the top trade destination in goods exports. Elsewhere the status cue may track aggregate flows, services exports, or even imports. It all depends on what each country considers more important. It is understandable that a service-oriented country like Malta will weight service trade more than a country like Brazil, where goods exports are what matters.\n317-\n318-To better understand variation in the media coverage on this, a supplemental source audit in the appendix uses displaced-incumbent coverage to check evidence of coverage in other countries. Australia illustrates a metric distinction. The *Australian Financial Review* reported on 9 November 2009 that China was Australia's largest aggregate trading partner, combining exports and imports of goods and services. The current goods-only annual panel codes Australia's first qualifying China-top goods-export year as 2009, after Japan held the top goods-export rank in 2008. The AFR item therefore documents broad-metric salience during the same annual treatment window; it does not establish that a strict goods-export cue preceded the 2009 treatment. This mismatch introduces measurement error into the cross-country treatment indicator. If this error is random and nondifferential with respect to potential voting outcomes and their trends, I would expect attenuation toward zero and would therefore interpret the estimated magnitude as conservative under that assumption.\n319-\n320-For the cross-country evidence beyond Brazil, the treated sample is restricted to what I call \"durable periods\" of China-top status. A durable period is one in which China is the largest goods-export destination for at least five consecutive observed years, after an observed prior year in which China was not number one.\n321-\n322-Since I am interested in a rising power becoming number one, specifically China, I restrict the treatment to years in or after 2000, when it is more plausible that China was seen as a rising power and the status cue would trigger a foreign policy change.\n323-\n324-Countries that never reach China-top goods-export status remain controls. I do allow for a country to switch into and out of treatment. Such cases remain in the sample. For treated countries, the main risk set keeps clean pre-entry non-China-top years and qualifying treated years, while excluding post-exit off-status years and short China-top episodes. This operationalization matches the salience logic more closely than either a one-year entry definition or an absorbing treatment rule: the theory expects political visibility, bureaucratic learning, and diplomatic adaptation to be clearer when the new trade hierarchy persists and is currently true. I compute partner ranks from ITPD-E goods sectors only, excluding services before ranks are constructed. I estimate this panel with the `fect` implementation and refer to it as the IFE specification below. The appendix reports duration-threshold and risk-set robustness checks.\n325-\n326-# Brazil SDiD Evidence\n327-\n328-The Brazilian analysis estimates the average post-2009 reduced-form effect of China's entry into the top export-destination position on Brazil's UNGA ideal-point distance to China. It asks whether Brazil moved closer to China than a synthetic counterfactual after the 2009 rank reversal.\n329-\n330-Figure \\@ref(fig:brazil-rank-volume) shows the timing of the rank threshold. China's export share rose before 2009, but China became the top-ranked export destination only in 2009. The signed margin over the relevant competitor also changes sign at that threshold.\n331-\n332-```{r brazil-rank-volume, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Brazil rank-versus-volume diagnostic for China's export-destination status. Panels show China's export share, ordinal rank, and signed margin over the relevant competitor. The dashed line marks the 2009 rank reversal. \", caption_note(unit = \"Export share in percent; ordinal rank; export-value margin in current USD billions\", window = \"2000-2012 annual Brazil series\")), fig.pos=\"H\", fig.width=7.2, fig.height=7.2}\n--\n736-commodity_mining_pct <- as.numeric(stringr::str_match(\n737-  commodity_detail, \"mining/energy=([0-9.]+)%\"\n738-)[, 2])\n739-stopifnot(\n740-  !is.na(commodity_primary_pct),\n741-  !is.na(commodity_agriculture_pct),\n742-  !is.na(commodity_mining_pct),\n743-  abs(commodity_agriculture_pct + commodity_mining_pct -\n744-        commodity_primary_pct) < 0.05\n745-)\n746-```\n747-\n748-Figure \\@ref(fig:rival-hypothesis) provides little support for the continuous-exposure account. Panel A measures exposure using goods trade, matching the sectoral definition used to assign treatment, while Panel B uses all-sector trade, including both goods and services. The relationship is essentially flat under both measures. The OLS slopes are `r sprintf('%.4f', dose_response_summary$primary_goods_delta_share_ols_slope_per_percentage_point)` and `r sprintf('%.4f', dose_response_summary$robustness_all_sector_delta_share_ols_slope_per_percentage_point)` per percentage point, respectively, while the corresponding Spearman correlations are `r sprintf('%.3f', dose_response_summary$primary_goods_delta_share_spearman_correlation)` and `r sprintf('%.3f', dose_response_summary$robustness_all_sector_delta_share_spearman_correlation)`. Under either measure, Brazil's ATT is also more negative than those of `r dose_donors_beaten` of the `r dose_high_dose_donors` donors in the highest quartile of exposure growth. These patterns do not rule out a role for continuous export dependence, nor do they identify a discontinuity at the rank threshold itself, but they make it less plausible that Brazil's result is driven simply by rising trade exposure to China.\n749-\n750-```{r rival-hypothesis, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"Trade-exposure dose and placebo-in-space pseudo-ATTs among \", dose_response_summary$n_donors, \" donor countries. Each donor is assigned the same 2009 pseudo-treatment, and dose is the difference between its mean annual export share directed to China in 2009--2015 and 1997--2008. Panel A measures goods trade; Panel B measures all-sector trade, including goods and services. Negative pseudo-ATTs indicate convergence toward China. Dark points identify donors in the top quartile of dose, vertical dotted lines mark the corresponding cutoffs, and black lines are descriptive OLS fits. The orange dashed horizontal line marks Brazil's ATT of \", sprintf('%.3f', dose_response_summary$brazil_estimate), \"; Brazil has no dose coordinate and therefore does not enter either fit. No donor had China as its largest goods-export destination during 1997--2015, so the figure shows only the dose gradient below the rank-one threshold and does not identify a discontinuity at that threshold. Donor pseudo-ATTs are estimated from overlapping pools, so no standard error is reported for either slope.\"), fig.pos=\"!htbp\", out.width=\"100%\"}\n751-knitr::include_graphics(\"images/figure_brazil_sdid_dose_response_panel.pdf\")\n752-```\n753-\n754:A remaining concern is that 2009 bundled the Brazilian rank reversal with other contemporaneous shocks, including the global financial crisis, the BRICS/G20 moment, and the commodity cycle. Table \\@ref(tab:china-demand-sdid-diagnostics) uses a measure built from external goods exports only to address this critique. Brazil's 2004--2008 primary-goods share is `r sprintf('%.2f', commodity_primary_pct)` percent, composed of `r sprintf('%.2f', commodity_agriculture_pct)` percent Agriculture and `r sprintf('%.2f', commodity_mining_pct)` percent Mining and Energy. The preferred ATT is `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", china_demand_main$estimate)`; adding primary composition and its 2008--2009 interaction yields `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", china_demand_commodity$estimate)`. Composition, decomposition, global-price, and prior-China-exposure specifications remain in a narrow negative range. The 2008--2009 interactions are robustness or mechanism diagnostics, not alternative total-effect estimates, because 2009 is the first treated year and the interaction may absorb part of the mechanism. Stability across these measures does not isolate the rank cue experimentally from every 2009 political shock, but it reduces the narrower concern that the preferred estimate is an artifact of post-treatment controls or omitted pre-2009 commodity composition.\n755-\n756-The cross-country panel below is a broader scope check, not the main answer to the Brazil-specific demand-shock critique. Brazil is included in the goods-only cross-country sample, but the panel aligns countries by their own China-top goods-export entry years. A Brazil-specific 2009 confounder cannot mechanically generate an average event-time effect across countries treated in different calendar years. For such an explanation to account for both the Brazil estimate and the cross-country result, the omitted force would need to coincide with durable China-top entry across countries, affect UNGA distance to China in the same direction, and remain outside the latent factors captured by the interactive fixed-effects specification. It would also need to be strongly enough associated with both treatment timing and the outcome to overturn estimates that are stable across the Brazil timing tests, donor-pool checks, and the cross-country duration and risk-set checks.\n757-\n758-```{r table-placebo, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\n759-rank_tests <- sdid_timing_placebos %>%\n760-  dplyr::mutate(\n761-    `Timing year` = nominal_treatment_year,\n762-    `Test role` = test_role,\n763-    `China rank` = china_rank,\n764-    `Export share (\\\\%)` = sprintf(\"%.1f\", china_share_pct),\n765-    `Margin (USD bn)` = sprintf(\"%.1f\", china_margin_vs_competitor_usd_billion),\n766-    ATT = sprintf(\"%.3f\", estimate),\n767-    Inference = inference\n768-  ) %>%\n769-  dplyr::select(\n770-    `Timing year`, `Test role`, `China rank`,\n771-    `Export share (\\\\%)`, `Margin (USD bn)`, ATT, Inference\n772-  )\n--\n959-```\n960-\n961-In that domain, Brazil-China identical voting increased by `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", human_rights_vote_change)` percentage points after 2009. This pattern is consistent with the proposed mechanism because it locates the clearest movement in a visible multilateral arena where Brazil had previously maintained more distance. It does not, however, show that the trade-rank cue itself caused the selective shift or that policymakers relied on it in particular decisions.\n962-\n963-The strongest support comes from human-rights resolutions in which China and the United States diverged, where Brazil moves closer to China relative to comparable donor countries after 2009.\n964-\n965-This descriptive increase does not by itself establish China-specific alignment, because annual issue-area shares can reflect agenda composition and do not compare Brazil with donor countries. To address such issues, I ran regressions with country and resolution fixed effects, restricted to human-rights votes where China and the United States cast different votes.\n966-\n967-The results show that Brazil moves closer to China after 2009 relative to the donor pool. The Brazil-by-post-2009 coefficient is `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_china_us_diff$estimate)` for distance to China minus distance to the United States (model-based SE = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_china_us_diff$se)`), and `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_china_us_strong_diff$estimate)` when the sample is restricted to strong yes/no China-US disagreements (model-based SE = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_china_us_strong_diff$se)`). The corresponding non-human-rights estimate is smaller (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", nonhr_china_us_diff$estimate)`, model-based SE = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", nonhr_china_us_diff$se)`). These are not causal estimates, given the usual selection and omitted-variable concerns.\n968-\n969-However, restricting attention to a single issue domain opens a design that ideal points computed over all votes cannot support: a triple difference. Ideal points compress every resolution in a session into one score, leaving no contrast between issue domains to exploit, whereas vote-level data preserve it. Thus, I can not only compare the difference between Brazil and other untreated countries before and after (traditional DiD), but also include a third difference: across domains within the same country and year, comparing Brazil with the donor pool, before and after 2009, in human-rights votes relative to every other vote. Identification here asks for more than parallel trends between treated and control units: whatever bias a non-parallel trend introduces must be the same in the two issue domains, so that it cancels in the third difference [@olden_moen2022]. Appendix Figure \\@ref(fig:plot-ddd-pretrends) displays the pre- and post-2009 Brazil-donor gaps in both domains; this descriptive comparison does not establish the identifying restriction.\n970-\n971-Let $B_i$ identify Brazil, $P_r$ indicate 2009--2012, and $H_r$ identify a human-rights resolution. The vote-level specification is\n972-\\[\n973-Y_{ir}=\\alpha_i+\\lambda_r+\\beta(B_iP_r)+\\gamma(B_iH_r)+\\delta(B_iP_rH_r)+\\varepsilon_{ir}.\n974-\\]\n975-Country and resolution fixed effects absorb the remaining lower-order terms. The Brazil-by-domain interaction allows Brazil's pre-existing gap relative to donors to differ between human-rights and other resolutions. The coefficient $\\delta$ measures the additional post-2009 human-rights shift. The sample contains `r format(ddd_hr_increment$n_obs, big.mark = \",\")` observed votes by `r ddd_hr_increment$n_countries` countries on `r ddd_hr_increment$n_resolutions` China-US divergent resolutions in 2005--2012. Any human-rights tag places a resolution in the human-rights group; all remaining resolutions form the non-human-rights group. Each observed country-resolution vote receives equal weight. The specification does not absorb a separate domain intercept for each donor country; interpretation also requires changes in donor vote availability not to generate the differential shift across domains.\n976-\n977:The DDD estimate is `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ddd_hr_increment$estimate)` for distance to China minus distance to the United States (country-clustered model-based SE = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ddd_hr_increment$se)`). Its negative sign indicates a larger shift toward China in human-rights votes. With Brazil as the single treated country, I also reassign the treated-country role to each country, rebuilding all three interactions and retaining the same sample and fixed effects. In this DDD-specific distribution, Brazil ranks `r sprintf(\"%.0f\", ddd_distance_placebo$brazil_rank_expected_direction)` of `r sprintf(\"%.0f\", ddd_distance_placebo$n_placebo_units)` in the predicted direction (inclusive directional tail share = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ddd_distance_placebo$randomization_p_directional)`; strict donor-only share = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ddd_distance_placebo$randomization_p_directional_strict_donor)`; two-sided absolute share = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", ddd_distance_placebo$randomization_p_two_sided)`). These are descriptive reassignment benchmarks; interpreting them as randomization inference requires exchangeability of country assignment.\n978-\n979-The separate human-rights-only DiD estimate of `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_only_distance_placebo$brazil_estimate)` ranks `r sprintf(\"%.0f\", hr_only_distance_placebo$brazil_rank_expected_direction)` of `r sprintf(\"%.0f\", hr_only_distance_placebo$n_placebo_units)` in its own country-placebo distribution (inclusive directional share = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_only_distance_placebo$randomization_p_directional)`; strict donor-only share = `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", hr_only_distance_placebo$randomization_p_directional_strict_donor)`). This benchmark evaluates the within-domain post-2009 contrast, whereas the DDD benchmark evaluates the additional shift across domains.\n980-\n981-```{r issue-area-prepost-table, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\n982-issue_order <- c(\n983-  \"Human rights\",\n984-  \"Arms/disarmament/nuclear\",\n985-  \"Economic development\",\n986-  \"Decolonization\",\n987-  \"Palestine/Middle East\",\n988-  \"Other / uncoded\"\n989-)\n990-\n991-issue_prepost_tbl <- tar_read(brazil_china_unvotes_resolution_2005_2012) %>%\n992-  dplyr::mutate(period = dplyr::if_else(year < 2009, \"pre\", \"post\")) %>%\n993-  dplyr::summarise(\n994-    n_resolutions = dplyr::n_distinct(rcid),\n995-    identical_vote_share = 100 * mean(identical_vote, na.rm = TRUE),\n--\n1726-```\n1727-\n1728-I also fit a model for a donor pool of Latin American states (excluding Caribbean countries), which is substantively attractive because the donor countries are regionally closer to Brazil. This regional donor pool is a sensitivity check rather than the preferred specification: the main design seeks a pre-treatment synthetic counterfactual with adequate donor support and fit, while the Latin American pool is much smaller.\n1729-\n1730-\n1731-```{r plot-latam, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap=paste0(\"SDiD fit with a Latin American donor pool, estimated without covariates. \", caption_note(unit = \"Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance to China\", se = paste0(sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$se_placebo[[1]]), \" (placebo, \", format(as.integer(sdid_latam_core_summary$se_replications[[1]]), big.mark = \",\", scientific = FALSE, trim = TRUE), \" replications)\"), window = \"1997-2015 annual series\")), fig.pos=\"H\", out.width=\"95%\"}\n1732-knitr::include_graphics(file.path(\n1733-  paper_sdid_figure_dir,\n1734-  \"figure_brazil_sdid_predetermined_core_latam_fit.png\"\n1735-))\n1736-b_est_main <- sdid_fit_summary$estimate[[1]]\n1737-b_est_latam <- sdid_latam_core_summary$estimate[[1]]\n1738-```\n1739-\n1740-Estimated without covariates, the Latin America donor-pool estimate has the same sign and a somewhat larger magnitude: `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", b_est_latam)`, compared with `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", b_est_main)` in the global donor pool. The regional pool has only `r sdid_latam_core_summary$n_donors[[1]]` donors and a slightly higher intercept-adjusted pre-treatment RMSPE (`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$rmspe_pre[[1]])` versus `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_fit_summary$rmspe_pre[[1]])`). Its placebo SE is `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$se_placebo[[1]])`, based on `r format(as.integer(sdid_latam_core_summary$se_replications[[1]]), big.mark = \",\", scientific = FALSE, trim = TRUE)` replications, and the normal-approximation 95 percent interval [`r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$ci_95_low[[1]])`, `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$ci_95_high[[1]])`] includes zero. Because the directional placebo comparison is computed within this regional pool, which contains only `r sdid_latam_core_summary$n_donors[[1]]` donor units, even Brazil being the `r scales::ordinal(sdid_latam_core_summary$rank_one_sided_negative[[1]])` most negative among all `r sdid_latam_core_summary$rank_denominator[[1]]` assignments yields a p-value of only `r sprintf(\"%.3f\", sdid_latam_core_summary$p_rank_one_sided_negative[[1]])`. In other words, the regional donor pool does not provide enough power to estimate the effect precisely. The point estimate is nevertheless consistent with the main result and, if anything, is somewhat larger in absolute value than the estimate from the larger global donor pool.\n1741-\n1742-## Cross-Country Robustness: Duration Thresholds\n1743-\n1744:The main cross-country estimate uses a five-year durability threshold because the theory concerns status that persists long enough to become politically usable. Table \\@ref(tab:cross-country-duration-table) reports the same goods-only IFE design under three- and seven-year thresholds, along with the clean single-entry robustness sample. The estimates remain negative across the restricted-risk-set rows, though precision varies with treated-unit support.\n1745-\n1746-```{r cross-country-duration-table, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}\n1747-duration_tbl <- m2_model_results %>%\n1748-  dplyr::filter(specification %in% c(\"risk_set_restricted\", \"clean_single_spell\")) %>%\n1749-  dplyr::mutate(\n1750-    `Minimum duration` = as.character(min_duration_years),\n1751-    Specification = specification_label,\n1752-    `ATT` = fmt3(att),\n1753-    `SE` = fmt3(se),\n1754-    `95\\\\% CI` = paste0(\"[\", fmt3(ci_lo), \", \", fmt3(ci_hi), \"]\"),\n1755-    `p-value` = vapply(p, format_p, character(1)),\n1756-    `Units (T/C)` = paste0(n_treated, \"/\", n_control),\n1757-    `Treated periods` = as.character(n_treated_country_years),\n1758-    `r*` = as.character(r_cv)\n1759-  ) %>%\n1760-  dplyr::transmute(\n1761-    `Minimum duration`,\n1762-    Specification,\n--\n2105-    general = sub(\n2106-      \"^Note: \",\n2107-      \"\",\n2108-      caption_note(\n2109-        unit = \"headline-level counts and accuracy (percent) by category\",\n2110-        window = \"stratified validation sample from the 2000-2014 headline corpus\"\n2111-      )\n2112-    ),\n2113-    general_title = \"Note: \",\n2114-    escape = FALSE,\n2115-    threeparttable = TRUE\n2116-  )\n2117-```\n2118-\n2119-The overall accuracy is `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", overall_acc)` percent. The lowest agreement is in the \"`r lowest_category`\" category (`r sprintf(\"%.1f\", lowest_acc)` percent), where several headlines were manually reclassified as \"diplomacy.\" The category most central to the mechanism, \"china-brazil trade,\" achieved `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", trade_acc)` percent agreement. Related bilateral categories had lower but still usable agreement: `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", relations_acc)` percent for \"china-brazil relations\" and `r sprintf(\"%.1f\", diplomacy_acc)` percent for \"diplomacy.\"\n2120-\n2121-## Cross-Country: Goods-Only Treated Countries\n2122-\n2123:Table \\@ref(tab:table-treated-appendix) describes the `r n_treated_abs` treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set specification. For each country, the table reports the first qualifying China-top goods-export year and the number of treated and untreated country-years retained in the estimation panel.\n2124-\n2125-```{r table-treated-appendix, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE}\n2126-library(kableExtra)\n2127-\n2128-treated_tbl <- m2_unit_summary %>%\n2129-  dplyr::filter(\n2130-    min_duration_years == 5L,\n2131-    sample == \"risk_set_restricted\",\n2132-    ever_treated\n2133-  ) %>%\n2134-  dplyr::arrange(first_treat, country_name) %>%\n2135-  dplyr::transmute(\n2136-    country = country_name,\n2137-    iso3c,\n2138-    first_treat_year = as.integer(first_treat),\n2139-    treated_years,\n2140-    untreated_years,\n2141-    first_year_in_panel,\n2142-    last_year_in_panel\n2143-  )\n2144-\n2145-kable(treated_tbl,\n2146-      format = \"latex\",\n2147-      booktabs = TRUE,\n2148-      digits = 3,\n2149:      caption = \"Treated countries in the main goods-only restricted-risk-set cross-country specification.\",\n2150:      col.names = c(\"Country\", \"ISO3c\", \"Treatment year\", \"Treated years\",\n2151:                     \"Pre/other untreated years\", \"First panel year\", \"Last panel year\"),\n2152-      align = \"llccccc\") %>%\n2153-  kable_styling(latex_options = c(\"hold_position\", \"scale_down\"),\n2154-                font_size = 9) %>%\n2155-  footnote(general = paste0(\"Ranks are computed from goods exports only. \",\n2156-                            \"The restricted risk set keeps clean pre-entry non-China-top years and qualifying treated years for treated countries. \",\n2157:                            \"Treatment year is the first retained year of a qualifying China-top goods-export period.\"),\n2158-           general_title = \"Note: \",\n2159-           escape = FALSE,\n2160-           threeparttable = TRUE)\n2161-```\n2162-\n2163-## Measurement Robustness: UNGA-DM Ideal Points\n2164-\n2165-```{r ungadm-inputs, include=FALSE}\n2166-ungadm_est_dir <- file.path(\n2167-  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"estimation\"\n2168-)\n2169-ungadm_post_dir <- file.path(\n2170-  \"data\", \"processed\", \"diagnostics\", \"ungadm_outcome_robustness\", \"postreview\"\n2171-)\n2172-\n2173-ungadm_sdid_comparison <- readr::read_csv(\n2174-  file.path(ungadm_est_dir, \"sdid_comparison_table.csv\"), show_col_types = FALSE\n2175-)\n--- table 23 in frozen PDF text ---\n2288-\n2289-                                                           56\n2290-\fare not automatically interpreted as showing that the cue was absent. Non-recovery may\n2291-instead reflect limited access to contemporaneous records many years after the event,\n2292-especially in countries where official speeches and news archives are less systematically\n2293-preserved or digitized. Table 21 reports the Australian source in full.\n2294-                                    Table 20: Recoverability and met