sample restrictions (p-value between 0.003 and 0.019). The panel evidence identifies a directional pattern associated with entry into the goods-only rank condition; the public-status mechanism is measured directly only in the Brazilian case.\\n\\nThe paper's contribution is to show that economic status matters and impacts foreign policy. Existing work usually treats trade exposure as a smooth source of influence. I argue that accumulated trade growth can become politically consequential when it crosses a durable, publicly legible rank threshold. It is particularly likely when a great power, such as China, holds the new status. In the case of Brazil, China's rise to the top export destination position made an already expanding commercial relationship available in public and official language as evidence of China's new centrality. The mechanism evidence shows that the public cue was available and used by policymakers in their political discourse, working as a focal point.\\n\\nThe next section develops the status-threshold argument and derives observable implications. I then present the Brazil SDiD design and timing diagnostics, followed by issue-area and media evidence on the mechanism. The final empirical section evaluates whether durable China-top goods-export status is associated with similar movement in a broader cross-country panel.\",\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-36-      \"label\": \"Human Written\",\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-37-      \"ai_assistance_score\": 0.027958476915955544,\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-38-      \"confidence\": \"Medium\",\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-39-      \"start_index\": 5063,\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-40-      \"end_index\": 7294,\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-41-      \"word_count\": 358,\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-42-      \"token_length\": 433\nquality_reports/2026-09-07_pangram_intro_only_raw.json-43-    }\n--\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-618-2. O check \"Frozen/static covariates are inert\" sobreviverá ao rebuild dizendo TRUE, porque continuará comparando o par errado. Se o autor pretende manter esse pacote como artefato de replicação, vale reformular o check para o par certo (`predetermined_core` com X vs sem X) ou renomeá-lo para o que de fato testa (\"Adding further predetermined covariates does not change the estimate\").\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-619-3. O check vizinho — \"Predetermined core reproduces no-time-varying target ATT, Local=-0.27230386; target=-0.27230386\" — **vai passar a falhar** assim que o pipeline for reconstruído, porque o target passará a valer -0.27200743. Item para a lista do rebuild noturno.\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-620-\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-621-### H-7 — Figuras 5, 13 e 14 vêm da especificação antiga [NOVO, IMPORTANTE]\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-622-\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-623-Os três `include_graphics` do Brazil SDiD apontam para arquivos gerados na execução **com** covariáveis:\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-624-\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-625-```\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md:626:quality_reports/china_demand_shock_rank_threshold/figure_brazil_sdid_predetermined_core_fit.png        14 jul 12:17\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-627-quality_reports/china_demand_shock_rank_threshold/figure_brazil_sdid_predetermined_core_weights.png    14 jul 12:17\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-628-quality_reports/china_demand_shock_rank_threshold/figure_brazil_sdid_predetermined_core_latam_fit.png  14 jul 12:17\nquality_reports/2026-08-23_audit_sdid_no_covariates.md-629-```\n