           # agrega se aparecer em >1 trigram\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-342-  summarise(n = sum(n), .groups = \"drop\") %>%\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-343-  filter(n > 5)                        # seu corte\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-344-\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-345-## 3. Criar grafo --------------------------------------------------------\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-346-trigram_graph <- graph_from_data_frame(edges, directed = FALSE)\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-347-\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-348-## 4. Plotar -------------------------------------------------------------\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R:349:a <- grid::arrow(type = \"closed\", length = unit(.2, \"inches\"))\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-350-ggraph(trigram_graph, layout = \"fr\") +\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-351-  geom_edge_link(aes(edge_alpha = n),\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R:352:                 arrow     = a,\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-353-                 end_cap   = circle(.07, 'inches'),\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-354-                 show.legend = FALSE) +\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-355-  geom_node_point(color = \"lightblue\", size = 3) +\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-356-  geom_node_text(aes(label = name), vjust = 1, hjust = 1) +\nscripts/explor_bigrams.R-357-  theme_void()\n--\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-316-| Structural bandwagoning | Persistent movement toward China after its rise | Exit analysis tests whether effects attenuate when China loses top rank | Reversibility is suggestive, not definitive |\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-317-| Chinese coercion | Alignment shifts due to pressure or inducements, not salience | Brazil is less vulnerable to simple coercion; panel patterns are evaluated through timing | Coercion and status may coexist |\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-318-| Domestic ideology/leader change | Alignment changes with government ideology | Brazil SDiD uses counterfactual timing; panel includes country and year structure | Country-specific political shifts may coincide with rank reversal |\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-319-```\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-320-\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-321-**Substituir parágrafo de interpretação por:**\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-322-\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-323-```markdown\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md:324:These diagnostics narrow the set of plausible alternatives but do not eliminate them. The Brazilian evidence is strongest for distinguishing rank salience from smooth trade growth because it combines timing placebos, trade covariates, and mechanism evidence from media coverage. The cross-country evidence is more cautious: it shows that periods under China top-export-destination status are associated with lower counterfactual UNGA distance to China, but it cannot fully separate ordinal rank from continuous export dependence in every country.\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-325-```\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-326-\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-327-### 15. Conclusão\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-328-\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-329-**Onde**: `paper_v4.Rmd`, linhas 841-849.\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-330-\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-331-**Substituir por:**\nquality_reports/2026-05-17_cross_country_rewrite_proposed_text.md-332-\n--\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-325-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-326-(se = sqrt(vcov(tau.hat, method = 'placebo')))\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-327-# coef\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-328-tau.hat[1]\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-329-z_score <- tau.hat[1]/se\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-330-pnorm(z_score)\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-331-plot(tau.hat, treated.name='Brazil') \nscripts/synth_analysis.R-332-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R:333:synthdid_plot(tau.hat, facet.vertical=FALSE,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-334-              control.name='control', treated.name='BRA',\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-335-              lambda.comparable=TRUE, se.method = 'none',\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-336-              trajectory.linetype = 1, line.width=.75, effect.curvature=-.4,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-337-              trajectory.alpha=.7, effect.alpha=.7,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-338-              diagram.alpha=1, onset.alpha=.7) \nscripts/synth_analysis.R-339-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-340-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-341-estimates <- lapply(estimators, function(estimator) { estimator(setup$Y,\n--\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-346-standard.errors = mapply(function(estimate, name) {\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-347-  set.seed(12345)\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-348-  if(name == 'mc') { mc_placebo_se(setup$Y, setup$N0, setup$T0) }\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-349-  else {             sqrt(vcov(estimate, method='placebo'))     }\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-350-}, estimates, names(estimators))\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-351-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-352-head(standard.errors)\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-353-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R:354:synthdid_plot(estimates[1], facet.vertical=FALSE,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-355-              control.name='control', treated.name='BRA',\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-356-              lambda.comparable=TRUE, se.method = 'none',\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-357-              trajectory.linetype = 1, line.width=.75, effect.curvature=-.4,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-358-              trajectory.alpha=.7, effect.alpha=.7,\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-359-              diagram.alpha=1, onset.alpha=.7) \nscripts/synth_analysis.R-360-\nscripts/synth_analysis.R-361-synthdid_units_plot(rev(estimates[1]), se.method='none') \nscripts/synth_analysis.R-362-\n--\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-251-<p>The reports are wrong or strategically harmful where they imply the\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-252-paper should abandon its core design, add broad policy trade-off\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-253-analysis, or concede that the paper is inherently not APSR-suitable. Do\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-254-not turn the conclusion into a cost-benefit essay. Do not bury the\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-255-Brazil design under a long list of secondary estimators. Do not remove\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-256-all “foreign-policy alignment” language; instead, discipline it by\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-257-repeatedly tying the empirical claim to “UNGA voting convergence toward\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-258-China, one observable dimension of foreign-policy alignment.”</p>\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html:259:<p>The revision logic is: make the contribution narrower but sharper.\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-260-The paper should become a theory of threshold salience in international\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-261-political economy: continuous trade growth matters, but crossing a\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-262-publicly legible rank threshold can reclassify a rising power from\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-263-“important partner” to “top partner,” especially when it displaces the\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-264-United States. Brazil supplies the high-salience case with the strongest\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-265-identification and media evidence; the cross-country panel supplies a\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-266-scope probe, not dispositive proof.</p>\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-267-<h2 id=\"executable-revision-goals\">Executable Revision Goals</h2>\n--\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-329-<hr />\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-330-<h3 id=\"goal-2-resolve-the-treatment-definition-problem-top-trade-partner-vs.-top-export-destination\">Goal\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-331-2 — Resolve the treatment-definition problem: top trade partner vs. top\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-332-export destination</h3>\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-333-<p><strong>Codex task:</strong> Audit and harmonize every treatment\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-334-definition in the manuscript, data construction, figures, captions, and\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-335-tables. Decide whether the main treatment is “top trade partner by total\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-336-bilateral trade” or “top export destination.” If the main panel must\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html:337:remain export-destination based, narrow the theory and wording\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-338-accordingly; if total-trade treatment is available and consistent with\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-339-the public salience claim, make it the main specification or a\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-340-co-primary robustness check.</p>\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-341-<p><strong>Actions:</strong></p>\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-342-<ol type=\"1\">\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-343-<li>Create treatment variants from the trade data: top export\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-344-destination, top import source, top total-trade partner, China’s ordinal\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-345-rank, China’s trade share, and China’s margin over the second-ranked\n--\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-519-<li>Add issue-area diagnostics that show whether the estimated\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-520-convergence is concentrated in human rights or also visible\nquality_reports/chatgpt_pro_revision_goals_paper_v4_20260517_1916.html-521-elsewhere.</li>\n--- baseline PDF text: identification and Figure 2 ---\n289-\ffor the main design because it is less sensitive to changes in the UNGA agenda over\n290-time. I use resolution-level agreement diagnostics only to interpret where convergence\n291-appears strongest. As a measurement-robustness check, the appendix re-estimates the\n292-main specifications using ideal points estimated by Fjelstul, Hug, and Kilby (2026), which\n293-are slightly different from Bailey, Strezhnev, and Voeten (2017) and based on another\n294-sample of resolution-related votes.\n295-\n296-For Brazil, the treatment indicator equals one from 2009 onward, the first year China be-\n297:came Brazil’s largest export destination. SDiD estimates Brazil’s average post-2009 gap\n298-relative to a weighted synthetic counterfactual, using pre-treatment information to construct\n299-unit and time weights (Arkhangelsky et al. 2021). The design is reduced-form. Its credibil-\n300-ity rests on pre-treatment fit, rank-versus-volume timing tests, donor-pool sensitivity, and\n301-outcome robustness.\n302-\n303-The SDiD panel combines UNGA ideal points with trade, macroeconomic, power, geo-\n304-graphic, institutional, and trade-agreement variables. The complete panel covers 96 coun-\n305-tries and 19 Brazil years for the main SDiD window (1997-2015). The preferred estimator\n--\n359-                        used to construct the synthetic counterfactual. The treatment mean is\n360-                        small because only Brazil is treated after 2009. Covariate summaries\n361-                        describe the full analytic panel; the preferred estimate uses none of\n362-                        them.\n363-\n364-\n365-\n366-\n367:3.2   Identification Strategy\n368-\n369:I want to estimate the causal effect of a country’s rise in China’s trade hierarchy on its\n370-foreign policy. The main direct challenge to causal identification is differentiating it from a\n371-continuous foreign-policy approximation as exposure to trade with China increases over\n372-\n373-\n374-                                                      10\n375-\ftime. Besides that, countries may move closer to China due to changes in domestic polit-\n376-ical coalitions or foreign-policy doctrine reorientation that coincide in timing with the treat-\n377-ment, because governments already inclined toward China deepen commercial ties before\n--\n474-threshold and risk-set robustness checks.\n475-\n476-\n477-\n478-\n479-                                             13\n480-\f4    Brazil SDiD Evidence\n481-\n482:The Brazilian analysis estimates the average post-2009 reduced-form effect of China’s en-\n483-try into the top export-destination position on Brazil’s UNGA ideal-point distance to China.\n484-It asks whether Brazil moved closer to China than a synthetic counterfactual after the 2009\n485-rank reversal.\n486-\n487-Figure 1 shows the timing of the rank threshold. China’s export share rose before 2009,\n488-but China became the top-ranked export destination only in 2009. The signed margin over\n489-the relevant competitor also changes sign at that threshold.\n490-\n--\n602-\n603-\n604-\n605-                         2000                     2005                       2010   2015\n606-\n607-\n608-\n609-\n610:Figure 2: Preferred SDiD fit for Brazil and its synthetic comparison, estimated without\n611:covariates. Lower values indicate convergence toward China; the post-treatment gap is\n612:the average post-2009 reduced-form estimate. Note: Absolute UNGA ideal-point distance\n613-to China. SE: placebo-based SE with 20,000 replications; p-values reported in the text\n614-and table. Window: 1997-2015 annual Brazil series.\n615-\n616-\n617-Figure 3 shows the placebo-in-space distribution behind that inference. Brazil sits in the\n618-left tail of the estimates obtained by reassigning treatment to each donor in turn.\n619-\n620-Table 2 summarizes the diagnostic package behind the preferred Brazil SDiD estimate.\n--- result file metadata ---\ndata/processed/diagnostics/paper_v4_brazil_sdid_no_covariates/main_summary.csv 749 bytes Aug 26 00:05:02 2026\ndata/processed/diagnostics/ungadm_outcome_robustness/estimation/sdid_comparison_table.csv 751 bytes Aug 26 05:32:38 2026\ndata/processed/diagnostics/ungadm_outcome_robustness/estimation/sdid_dm_main_summary.csv 779 bytes Aug 26 05:32:38 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
